#!/bin/sh
# vigilant -- cross an edge, and be vigilant that it took effect.
#
# vigilance's two pillars, in one command: make sure ALL the things go dark
# when they should, and make sure the mechanism that does it is WORKING. See
# docs/framework-refactor.md for the model this implements.
#
# FIXED VERBS, and the ARGUMENT IS THE STATE. The verb set is closed, so a new
# rung can never collide with a command name (which edge-as-verb could not
# promise):
#
#   vigilant go <state>       traverse to state, respecting the ladder
#   vigilant go <state> atleast   deepen to state; NEVER raise toward it
#   vigilant force <state>    assert state, ignoring the recorded depth
#   vigilant only <state>     cross ONE edge into state, no traversal
#   vigilant status           where we are, and where hooks live
#   vigilant verify [edge]    run reverse hooks; assert reality
#   vigilant hooks [edge]     what would run, in order, resolved
#   vigilant plan [edge]      the same, PER CONTEXT: session vs greeter
#   vigilant report           does reality match what we believe?
#   vigilant rescue           record everything, then recover
#   vigilant due [edge]       when SHOULD this edge have fired?
#   vigilant enforce          the supervision loop, for a timer
#   vigilant audit            did past events produce the edges they should?
#
# THERE IS NO `vigilant check`, deliberately. The wiring audit is `setup.sh
# check`, which is the only thing that knows PREFIX, the dependency list and
# the plugin tree it installed; a second implementation here would be the same
# fact in two places, which is the failure mode this project keeps finding.
#
# It used to exist as a stub aliased to `status` with a "stage 4 replaces this"
# comment, while three documents described it as a wiring audit. A command that
# silently answers a DIFFERENT question than its documentation is worse than a
# missing one: the missing one fails loudly. It now fails loudly, and says where
# the real audit lives.
#
# THE LADDER, one dimension: how far the machine has WITHDRAWN. Present tense
# throughout, so a state names a condition the machine is IN, never something
# that already happened to it:
#
#   open     fully here; unlocked and lit
#   lock     session secured; the screen is still lit
#   sleep    peripherals down (DDC standby, backlights 0, LEDs off)
#   suspend  machine in S3/hibernate
#
# `go` is DECLARATIVE: say where you want to be and every edge in between is
# crossed. So `go suspend` from `lock` fires sleep.d AND suspend.d, which is
# why suspending now actually powers the monitor down instead of leaving it
# lit. There is no minimum-rung special case; you simply cannot skip a rung.
#
# SEVEN HOOK KINDS, and vigilant hardcodes NO mechanism in any of them. It
# knows nothing of logind, swayidle, swaylock or systemd; each is a hook that
# binds vigilance to one environment. That is the whole point of the split:
#
#   <edge>.d          DO it        actuators (drive the hardware)
#   <edge>.verify.d   CHECK it     did the edge actually take effect
#   <edge>.report.d   TELL         announce the change outward (logind's
#                                  SetLockedHint, a bar, an indicator)
#   <edge>.due.d      WHEN         when SHOULD this edge have fired? The
#                                  timeout lives outside vigilant, so a hook
#                                  reads it (swaylock.conf, a swayidle timer)
#   <edge>.block.d    WHY NOT      is something legitimately preventing it
#                                  (an idle inhibitor, a spurious re-fire)?
#                                  Runs BEFORE the depth is committed, so a
#                                  blocked edge changes NOTHING (exit 3).
#                                  Exit 10 = BLOCK, anything else = allow.
#                                  FAIL OPEN by design: a broken block hook
#                                  must never be able to suppress a LOCK, so
#                                  only an explicit 10 blocks. Suppressing a
#                                  lock is a security failure; allowing a
#                                  redundant one is merely noise.
#   audit.d           DID IT       read an external event source and report
#                                  events that SHOULD have produced an edge.
#                                  Forensic, not live: the only kind that can
#                                  see an event which produced no edge at all.
#                                  NOT per-edge, like alert: the hook NAMES the
#                                  edge in its output, because one source (a
#                                  journal) reports events for several edges.
#   alert.d           SHOUT        tell the HUMAN something needed attention.
#                                  NOT per-edge: it is cross-cutting, raised
#                                  by vigilant itself
#
# THE THREE QUESTIONS, which is why verify/due/audit are separate kinds and not
# one cleverer kind. They differ in TENSE, and none can answer another:
#
#   verify  NOW   does the hardware match the rung we believe we are at?
#   due     SOON  is an edge pending and past its deadline?
#   audit   PAST  did an event that already happened produce its edge?
#
# LOG vs ALERT, deliberately different: the LOG is vigilant's own record and is
# built in, because a supervisor that only records when an integrator wires
# something up is not a supervisor. Alerting a human is POLICY (a toast, a bar,
# an intervention flag), so it is a hook and vigilant assumes no mechanism.
#
# `due` and `block` are read INWARD from the environment; `report` is written
# OUTWARD to it. Both directions are hooks, so playing nice with a stock
# mechanism never means hardcoding an assumption about which mechanism it is.
#
# EDGES are the transitions between rungs, and hooks are keyed by them. A
# DESCENT edge shares its name with the state it enters; only the ascent needs
# words of its own:
#
#   open -> lock     lock        lock -> open     unlock
#   lock -> sleep    sleep       sleep -> lock    wake
#   sleep -> suspend suspend     suspend -> sleep resume
#
# Ascent is NOT the descent reversed. Leaving `sleep` the saved state is valid,
# so a hook restores it; leaving `suspend` USB re-enumerated and a QMK board
# forgot everything, so the saved state is STALE and the hook must re-assert
# from config. Same hook, same direction, different correct behaviour, which is
# why the runner passes the rung being LEFT as $2/FROM.
#
# HARD CONSTRAINT: `go suspend` does NOT suspend. It fires the hooks for that
# edge, invoked BY the unit ordered Before=sleep.target. systemd stays the
# actuator; vigilant only crosses edges. The moment this initiates a power
# transition it competes with the trust root instead of riding it.
#
# EXIT CODES are a contract, because units and the patch branch on them:
#   0  ok, including a no-op
#   1  crossed, but one or more hooks FAILED (degraded, but we did move)
#   2  usage error
#   3  refused: a precondition was not met and NOTHING happened
set -eu

PKG=vigilant

# HOME is NOT guaranteed. A login shell has it; a systemd unit with no User=,
# a sleep hook under /lib/systemd/system-sleep, and cloud-init's runcmd do not,
# and under `set -u` an unset HOME is a hard exit 2 before anything runs. Since
# every edge crossing is invoked from exactly those contexts, derive it from
# the passwd database rather than assume it, and fail LOUDLY if even that
# cannot answer. (Caught by test/vm/run on its first boot.)
if [ -z "${HOME:-}" ]; then
  HOME=$(getent passwd "$(id -u)" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f6 || true)
  if [ -z "$HOME" ]; then
    echo "$PKG: HOME unset and not derivable from passwd" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  export HOME
fi

_cfg=${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}

# TWO SCOPES, because peripherals belong to the MACHINE and locking, audio and
# notifications belong to a USER.
#
#   machine  /etc/vigilance/hooks      root-owned, EVERY session
#   user     ~/.config/vigilance/hooks user-owned, no sudo ever
#
# This is what lets a greeter session work with no configuration of its own: it
# simply has no user root, so it gets the machine hooks and nothing else. The
# alternative (one shared tree) would need per-session filtering, which pushes
# session-awareness into the mechanism, and a greeter cannot read an encrypted
# user home anyway.
#
# ORDERING: machine is the LOWER LAYER, so it comes up first and goes down
# last, exactly as systemd orders units. Dependencies point user -> machine and
# never the reverse: a USB DAC powered down by a machine hook must be back
# before a user hook tries to unmute it by name.
#
#   descent  user 10->90, then machine 10->90
#   ascent   machine 90->10, then user 90->10
MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT=${VIGILANCE_MACHINE_HOOKS:-/etc/vigilance/hooks}
HOOK_ROOT=${VIGILANCE_HOOK_ROOT:-$_cfg/vigilance/hooks}
# Runtime state. XDG_RUNTIME_DIR survives suspend and hibernate but NOT a
# reboot, which is exactly right: a boot means we are at the top of the ladder.
#
# FINDING THE WRONG RUNTIME DIR IS ANSWERING ABOUT A DIFFERENT MACHINE, and the
# bare `${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp}` fallback did exactly that. The variable is
# unset in a non-interactive ssh, in cron, and under `su`, so `vigilant status`
# there read an empty /tmp/vigilance and reported `depth: open, since: unknown`
# -- the SAME answer whether the machine was open, locked or dark. A tool whose
# subject is truthful state, confidently wrong about it, with nothing saying the
# record it read was not the session's.
#
# The real one is still findable: logind creates /run/user/<uid> for any session
# of that uid, including the ssh one, and it is 0700 and ours. So look there
# before giving up, which FIXES the ssh case rather than merely reporting it.
#
# The last resort is PER-UID, under TMPDIR. `/tmp/vigilance` was a shared name
# in a sticky world-writable directory: whoever creates it first owns it, and a
# pre-planted symlink there would redirect the depth writes of everyone who came
# after. That is a poor place for the authoritative state of a lock supervisor,
# and a per-uid name costs nothing.
# The base is overridable for the same reason every other host read here is:
# a test asserting "no runtime dir was found" must be able to arrange that
# without depending on whether the developer's box happens to have one.
_rtbase=${VIGILANCE_RUNTIME_BASE:-/run/user}
_rt=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-}
_uid=$(id -u)
if [ -z "$_rt" ] && [ -d "$_rtbase/$_uid" ]; then _rt=$_rtbase/$_uid; fi
if [ -n "${VIGILANCE_RUN_DIR:-}" ]; then
  RUN_DIR=$VIGILANCE_RUN_DIR
elif [ -n "$_rt" ]; then
  RUN_DIR=$_rt/vigilance
else
  # TMPDIR is honoured, both because that is what it is for and because it is
  # what makes this branch testable inside a sandbox.
  # Recorded so `report` can say the state is not where the session keeps it.
  RUN_DIR=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/vigilance-$_uid
  RUN_DIR_FALLBACK=1
fi
DEPTH_FILE=$RUN_DIR/depth
STATE_ROOT=$RUN_DIR/state

# --- THE LADDER, DECLARED ONCE ---------------------------------------------
#
# Five functions each knew the vocabulary by heart -- _is_descent, _is_ascent,
# _intent_of, _verify_edge_for, _next_down -- plus five more lists scattered
# through plan, report and the fingerprint, and the rung list below written out
# a second time. Eleven places to edit in step, with nothing to notice if one
# was missed. That breaks the first house rule: ONE VALUE DRIVES ALL ITS
# DEPENDENTS, never the same fact in two places where they can drift.
#
#   rung    edge arriving DOWNWARD    edge arriving UPWARD    intent
#
# INTENT BELONGS TO THE RUNG, NOT THE EDGE, which is why this collapses at all.
# `resume` reads dark and `wake` reads lit purely because of where each LANDS:
# resume returns to the `sleep` rung with peripherals still down, wake climbs
# out to `lock`. Said per rung, every special case disappears.
#
# `-` means the ladder ends: nothing descends into `open`, nothing ascends out
# of `suspend`.
LADDER_TABLE='open - unlock lit
lock lock wake lit
sleep sleep resume dark
suspend suspend - dark'

# POLICY, NOT STRUCTURE, and separate for that reason. `suspend` is a real rung
# with a real edge; what it is not is an ENFORCEMENT target, because vigilant
# does not suspend -- systemd does -- so forcing it would prepare peripherals
# and never cross. Structure describes the machine; this says what we are
# willing to drive it to.
NEVER_ENFORCED='suspend'

# The cross-cutting tiers are NOT edges. Listing them beside edges is what once
# made an audit hook read as wired while nothing would ever run it.
CROSS_KINDS='audit alert idle watchdog'

_lt() { printf '%s\n' "$LADDER_TABLE"; }

_all_edges() {   # every edge name, descents then ascents
  _lt | awk '$2 != "-" { print $2 }'
  _lt | awk '$3 != "-" { print $3 }'
}

# FLATTENED ONCE AT STARTUP, then every lookup is pure shell.
#
# The first cut ran an awk per call, and _intent_of is called once per HOOK per
# edge: measured at 1210ms against 1128ms for a lock+unlock pair, ~7% on the
# security path. Noise against a 25s budget, but it was a regression this
# refactor had no business introducing -- single-sourcing is about where a fact
# is DECLARED, not about re-deriving it on every question.
# ONE awk PASS for all four, because four spawns per invocation is most of
# what the per-call version cost. The table is a literal in this file, so the
# eval has nothing external to trust.
eval "$(_lt | awk '
  { if ($2 != "-") { d = d $2 " "; m = m $2 "=" $4 " " }
    if ($3 != "-") { a = a $3 " "; m = m $3 "=" $4 " " }
    r = r sep $1; sep = " " }
  END { printf "DESCENT_EDGES=\"%s\"\n", d
        printf "ASCENT_EDGES=\"%s\"\n",  a
        printf "INTENT_MAP=\"%s\"\n",    m
        printf "LADDER=\"%s\"\n",        r }')"

_is_descent() {   # edge
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] || return 1
  case " $DESCENT_EDGES " in *" $1 "*) return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}

_is_ascent() {   # edge
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] || return 1
  case " $ASCENT_EDGES " in *" $1 "*) return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}


# GRACE: how far past a declared deadline an edge must be before supervision
# calls it overdue. It exists so the PRIMARY mechanism wins the race in the
# normal case -- vigilant is the backstop, not the timer, and acting on a tie
# would make it compete with the thing it is supervising.
GRACE=${VIGILANCE_GRACE:-30}

# vigilant's OWN record. Built in and mechanism-free (it is a file), because
# the whole charter is noticing when something silently did not happen, and a
# record that depends on an integrator wiring it up would be the first thing
# missing. stderr goes to the journal under a unit but NOWHERE under a
# keybind, which is exactly when we most want the trail.
LOG_FILE=${VIGILANCE_LOG:-${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/vigilance.log}

_log() {   # message
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LOG_FILE")" 2>/dev/null || true
  printf '%s %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S')" "$*" >> "$LOG_FILE" \
    2>/dev/null || true
}

log() { printf '%s: %s\n' "$PKG" "$*" >&2; _log "$*"; }

# PROVENANCE travels in the ENVIRONMENT, never in argv: `vigilant go lock`
# takes no arguments by contract, and depth stays the only authoritative
# state. VIGILANCE_SOURCE says WHO asked (idle, logind, lid, manual, suspend),
# which block hooks need and vigilant itself deliberately ignores.
#
# It is safety-critical that this stays hook policy. smart-lock debounced ONLY
# the idle path and never suspend/manual/Session.Lock, because gating the
# suspend path would sleep the box UNLOCKED. That distinction lives in the
# hook, which is the only thing that knows what a source means.
_blocked() {   # edge from -> 0 if an explicit block was raised
  _bl=$(_hooks_for "$1" .block)
  if [ -z "$_bl" ]; then return 1; fi
  _hit=1
  while IFS= read -r _bh; do
    if [ -z "$_bh" ]; then continue; fi
    _brc=0
    # BOUNDED, and this is the sharpest of the tiers to bound: block hooks run
    # BEFORE the depth is committed and before any actuator sees the edge, so a
    # hook that hangs here stops the lock earlier than one in lock.d does.
    # Measured unbounded: `go lock` sat for as long as the hook did and the
    # provider never ran.
    #
    # CAPTURED VIA A FILE, NOT `$(...)`, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART.
    # A command substitution waits for the PIPE to close, not for the hook to
    # exit. A hook that leaves a child holding stdout therefore blocks the
    # substitution for as long as the CHILD lives, and the bound is simply not
    # in force: measured at 21s against a 2s bound, with timeout(1) having
    # correctly killed the hook at 4s. Writing to a file removes the pipe, so
    # an orphan still writing afterwards delays nobody.
    #
    # It looks fine when the hook dies politely (2s, correct), which is exactly
    # why it survived: the broken and the working case are indistinguishable
    # unless the hook outlives its own children. The same shape applies to the
    # due and audit tiers, which capture the same way.
    mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true
    _bcf=$RUN_DIR/.block.$$
    VIGILANCE_EDGE="$1" VIGILANCE_FROM="$2" \
      $HOOK_TMO "$_bh" "$1" "$2" >"$_bcf" 2>/dev/null || _brc=$?
    _why=$(cat "$_bcf" 2>/dev/null || true)
    rm -f "$_bcf" 2>/dev/null || true
    case "$_brc" in
      10) log "BLOCKED $1 by $(basename "$_bh"): ${_why:-no reason given}"
          _hit=0 ;;
      0)  ;;
      124|137)
          log "block hook $(basename "$_bh") TIMED OUT; ALLOWING" ;;
      *)  log "block hook $(basename "$_bh") errored (rc=$_brc); ALLOWING" ;;
    esac
  done <<EOF
$_bl
EOF
  return "$_hit"
}

# _alert <kind> <message>: something a HUMAN should know about. Logged always,
# then handed to alert.d so the integrator decides how to surface it (a
# desktop toast, an intervention flag, a bar indicator). A failing alerter can
# never affect the outcome it is reporting on, so failures here are swallowed
# after being logged: the machine's state must not depend on whether we
# managed to complain about it.
# ALERT ON THE ONSET, NOT ON THE STATE. An identical alert raised again while
# the same condition persists is not new information, and a notifier that fires
# every minute about an unchanged fact is one people silence. That is not a
# guess: the enforce timer on a live box was STOPPED BY HAND to quiet an alert
# storm and then simply stayed stopped, with every subsequent report green.
#
# So a repeat of the SAME alert within the cooldown is logged and not notified.
# The LOG IS NEVER SUPPRESSED -- the forensic record has to stay complete, and
# it is the audit tier's input. Only the human-facing notification is throttled.
#
# Keyed on kind AND message, so a different fault about the same subsystem still
# gets through; the state lives in RUN_DIR, so a reboot re-arms everything,
# which is the right default for "is this still true".
ALERT_COOLDOWN=${VIGILANCE_ALERT_COOLDOWN:-3600}

_alert_repeat() {   # kind message -> 0 if this is a suppressible repeat
  if [ "$ALERT_COOLDOWN" = 0 ]; then return 1; fi
  # DURATIONS ARE NORMALISED OUT OF THE KEY, or the dedup is defeated by the
  # very alerts that need it most. A watchdog message says "emitted nothing for
  # 45252s" and a minute later "45317s": same finding, different text, so every
  # pass hashed as a fresh onset. Measured on a live box -- 39 notified, ZERO
  # suppressed -- which is the storm this function exists to prevent and the
  # documented reason an enforce timer once got stopped by hand.
  #
  # Only digit-runs followed by `s` are collapsed, so a measurement that
  # IDENTIFIES something stays distinct: "bus 4" and "bus 5" are different
  # findings, "45252s" and "45317s" are the same one told twice.
  _ak=$(printf '%s %s' "$1" "$2" \
        | sed 's/[0-9][0-9]*s/Ns/g' | cksum | tr -d ' ')
  _af=$RUN_DIR/alerts/$_ak
  mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR/alerts" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  if [ -f "$_af" ]; then
    _aw=$(cat "$_af" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    case "$_aw" in ''|*[!0-9]*) _aw=0 ;; esac
    if [ $(( $(date +%s) - _aw )) -lt "$ALERT_COOLDOWN" ]; then return 0; fi
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$(date +%s)" > "$_af" 2>/dev/null || true
  return 1
}

_alert() {   # kind message
  _log "ALERT [$1] $2"
  if _alert_repeat "$1" "$2"; then
    _log "  (repeat within ${ALERT_COOLDOWN}s; logged, not notified)"
    return 0
  fi
  _al=$(_hooks_for alert)
  if [ -z "$_al" ]; then return 0; fi
  while IFS= read -r _ah; do
    if [ -z "$_ah" ]; then continue; fi
    # BOUNDED, for the same reason every other hook is (see HOOK_TIMEOUT) and
    # with a sharper edge: _alert is reached FROM _run_hooks when a hook fails,
    # so a notifier that hangs blocks the very edge whose failure raised it. A
    # broken toast must not be able to stop a lock.
    VIGILANCE_ALERT_KIND="$1" VIGILANCE_ALERT_MSG="$2" \
      $HOOK_TMO "$_ah" "$1" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || _log "  alert hook failed: $(basename "$_ah")"
  done <<EOF
$_al
EOF
}

# The edge crossed by moving between ADJACENT rungs.
#
# Descending is named for the rung ENTERED, so a descent edge and its target
# state share a name: `go sleep` crosses the `sleep` edge into the `sleep`
# state, running sleep.d. One word, not two, and _edge_down is therefore just
# the rung name.
#
# Ascending is named for the rung LEFT, because that is the distinction that
# matters: leaving `sleep` the saved state is valid (wake, restore it), leaving
# `suspend` it is stale (resume, re-assert instead). Those names cannot collide
# with state names, which is why the ascent keeps its own three words.
_edge_down() { _rung_at "$1"; }        # index of the rung being ENTERED
_edge_up() {                           # index of the rung being LEFT
  case "$1" in 3) echo resume ;; 2) echo wake ;; 1) echo unlock ;; esac
}

# Only an ASCENT edge unwinds in reverse. Anything else (a descent, and the
# non-edge `alert` set) runs in plain ascending order.
_depth_of() {   # rung -> integer, or non-zero if it is not a rung
  _i=0
  for _r in $LADDER; do
    if [ "$_r" = "$1" ]; then echo "$_i"; return 0; fi
    _i=$((_i + 1))
  done
  return 1
}

_rung_at() {   # integer -> rung
  _i=0
  for _r in $LADDER; do
    if [ "$_i" -eq "$1" ]; then echo "$_r"; return 0; fi
    _i=$((_i + 1))
  done
  return 1
}

# The depth file is "<rung> <epoch>": the rung, and WHEN it was entered.
# The timestamp is what makes supervision possible at all -- without it there
# is no deadline to compute for a `due` hook to be measured against. A legacy
# file holding only a rung reads back with epoch 0, which simply means "no
# deadline can be computed yet" rather than an error.
# A session may BEGIN at a rung rather than at the top. A greeter IS the locked
# state: there is no user session to protect, so it starts at `lock` and idles
# down to `sleep` from there.
#
# This is an INITIALISATION, not a crossing: no hooks fire, because nothing was
# traversed. Without it a greeter's `go sleep` would traverse open -> lock ->
# sleep and fire lock.d, whose provider would try to start a lock screen on top
# of a session that is already one.
_depth() {   # the current rung
  if [ -r "$DEPTH_FILE" ]; then
    cut -d' ' -f1 < "$DEPTH_FILE"
  else
    _init=${VIGILANCE_INITIAL_DEPTH:-open}
    if _depth_of "$_init" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      echo "$_init"
    else
      log "ignoring bad VIGILANCE_INITIAL_DEPTH '$_init'; using 'open'"
      echo open
    fi
  fi
}

_depth_at() {   # epoch the current rung was entered, or 0
  # Guard on readability rather than redirecting stderr: a failed input
  # redirect is reported by the SHELL before a later 2>/dev/null can take
  # effect, so the error would leak to the terminal on a fresh run.
  if [ ! -r "$DEPTH_FILE" ]; then echo 0; return 0; fi
  _t=$(cut -d' ' -f2 < "$DEPTH_FILE" || true)
  case "${_t:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo 0 ;; *) echo "$_t" ;; esac
}

# ATOMIC REPLACE, because this file has several writers and a minutely reader.
#
# `> "$DEPTH_FILE"` TRUNCATES and only then writes, so a reader landing in that
# window gets an EMPTY file. Measured under contention, not theorised: 2 empty
# reads in 4000, reproducibly.
#
# An empty read is not a harmless blip. `_depth` returns nothing, `_depth_of ""`
# fails, and every caller does `_dfrom=$(_depth_of "$(_depth)") || _dfrom=0` --
# and 0 is `open`. So a reader concludes the machine is UNLOCKED while it is at
# `sleep`, and a traversal from that false start records `lock` while the
# peripherals stay dark with nothing armed to re-light them.
#
# Who races: the enforce timer reads this every minute forever, the audit timer
# daily, while Super+L, three swayidle timers, the suspend unit and the resume
# unit all write it. 1,440 attempts a day is not a rare event budget.
#
# A rename is atomic within one filesystem, so a reader sees the whole old value
# or the whole new one. The temp name carries the PID so two writers cannot
# clobber each other's partial file; the last rename wins, which is correct (a
# later crossing is a later truth) and is never TORN.
_set_depth() {   # rung
  _sd_tmp=$DEPTH_FILE.$$
  if printf '%s %s\n' "$1" "$(date +%s)" > "$_sd_tmp" \
     && mv -f "$_sd_tmp" "$DEPTH_FILE"; then
    return 0
  fi
  rm -f "$_sd_tmp" 2>/dev/null || true
  log "could not record depth '$1' at $DEPTH_FILE"
  return 1
}

# --- the crossing is IN FLIGHT ----------------------------------------------
# THE RECORD IS DELIBERATELY AHEAD OF THE MACHINE WHILE AN EDGE IS BEING
# CROSSED, and until now nothing said so.
#
# _cross_one commits the depth and THEN runs the act tier, which is the right
# order: a hook must be able to read the rung it is acting for, and a failure
# to write a file must never suppress the lock. But it means that between the
# commit and the provider finishing, `lock` is recorded while no locker is up
# yet -- asserted-vs-actual drift that is CORRECT and transient.
#
# Measured on manifestor, on a hotkey press:
#
#   23:51:13  vigilance-enforce.service starts (the standing recheck)
#   23:51:13  HOOK FAILED: lock.verify 50-locker-up
#   23:51:13  Starting screen-lock.service ... swaylock     <- after the verify
#   23:51:14  STILL-DRIFTED at 'lock': no locker is up
#
# Two alerts about a lock that was working. The existing guard cannot see this
# one: it discards a verdict when the depth MOVES during the verify, and here
# the depth was already committed and perfectly still. The window is not
# between two reads, it is between the record and the machine.
#
# A LEAKED MARKER MUST NEVER SILENTLY DISABLE THE TIER, which is the obvious
# way to make this cure worse than the disease: vigilant killed mid-crossing
# (systemd reaping lock-on-sleep on its 25s timeout is the realistic case)
# would otherwise leave a file that switches off the only check that watches a
# settled machine. So the marker is only believed while its writer is ALIVE,
# and capped in time as a backstop against a recycled pid.
CROSSING_MARK=$RUN_DIR/crossing
CROSSING_MAX=${VIGILANCE_CROSSING_MAX:-120}

_crossing_begin() {
  printf '%s %s\n' "$$" "$(date +%s)" > "$CROSSING_MARK" 2>/dev/null || true
}

_crossing_end() {
  rm -f "$CROSSING_MARK" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# WHEN DID SOMETHING LAST RAISE THE MACHINE? Recorded here rather than derived
# from the depth file, because that carries only the CURRENT rung and its entry
# time: once the box descends again the fact that an ascent happened is gone,
# and it is precisely across that descent that the ceiling still matters.
ASCENT_MARK=$RUN_DIR/last-ascent

_note_ascent() {
  printf '%s\n' "$(date +%s)" > "$ASCENT_MARK" 2>/dev/null || true
}

_last_ascent() {   # epoch of the last ascent, or 0
  if [ ! -r "$ASCENT_MARK" ]; then echo 0; return 0; fi
  _av=$(head -1 "$ASCENT_MARK" 2>/dev/null || true)
  case "${_av:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo 0 ;; *) echo "$_av" ;; esac
}

_crossing_inflight() {   # 0 if an edge is genuinely being crossed right now
  [ -r "$CROSSING_MARK" ] || return 1
  _cp=$(cut -d' ' -f1 < "$CROSSING_MARK" 2>/dev/null || true)
  _ct=$(cut -d' ' -f2 < "$CROSSING_MARK" 2>/dev/null || true)
  case "${_cp:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  case "${_ct:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  # Fail OPEN on every doubt. `kill -0` also fails for a process we do not own,
  # and the answer we want there is "judge the machine", not "stay silent".
  kill -0 "$_cp" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [ "$(( $(date +%s) - _ct ))" -le "$CROSSING_MAX" ] || return 1
  return 0
}

# --- hook enumeration -------------------------------------------------------
# Descent runs ascending (10 -> 90); ascent runs descending (90 -> 10), so a
# restore unwinds in the reverse order it wound down. Only executables count,
# which is the same contract smart-lock's lock-hooks already use.
# _hooks_in <root> <edge> <suffix> <asc|desc>: one root, ordered.
_hooks_in() {
  _hd=$1/$2$3.d
  if [ ! -d "$_hd" ]; then return 0; fi
  _hl=""
  for _hh in "$_hd"/*; do
    if [ -x "$_hh" ]; then _hl="$_hl$_hh
"; fi
  done
  if [ -z "$_hl" ]; then return 0; fi
  # Sort on the BASENAME so the numeric prefix orders within the scope,
  # independently of how long the root's path happens to be.
  if [ "$4" = desc ]; then
    printf '%s' "$_hl" | awk -F/ '{print $NF"\t"$0}' | sort -r | cut -f2-
  else
    printf '%s' "$_hl" | awk -F/ '{print $NF"\t"$0}' | sort | cut -f2-
  fi
}

_hooks_for() {   # edge [suffix]
  if _is_ascent "$1"; then
    # Coming UP: the lower layer first, each scope unwinding its own order.
    _hooks_in "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$1" "${2:-}" desc
    _hooks_in "$HOOK_ROOT" "$1" "${2:-}" desc
  elif [ "$1" = alert ] || [ "$1" = audit ] || [ "$1" = idle ] \
       || [ "$1" = watchdog ]; then
    # NEITHER IS A LADDER EDGE, so say so explicitly rather than letting them
    # fall through to the descent branch and be ordered as if they were one.
    # `audit` landed in that branch by accident; the order it produced happened
    # to be the same, which is exactly why it went unnoticed.
    #
    # Machine first so the always-available journal sink records BEFORE any user
    # sink that might be slow or absent: the record must not be the part lost.
    _hooks_in "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$1" "${2:-}" asc
    _hooks_in "$HOOK_ROOT" "$1" "${2:-}" asc
  else
    # Going DOWN: tear down the high layer first, leave the machine for last.
    _hooks_in "$HOOK_ROOT" "$1" "${2:-}" asc
    _hooks_in "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$1" "${2:-}" asc
  fi
}

# --- crossing an edge -------------------------------------------------------
# Every hook gets the edge, the rung being left, and a state dir the runner
# creates for it. That state dir is what deletes the copy-pasted save-file
# logic panel-power reimplemented three times over.
#
# A failing hook is LOUD and does NOT abort the rest: one dark monitor must not
# leave the keyboard lit. This is the direct fix for swaylock's `_exit(127)`,
# which was silent BY DESIGN and hid a dead hook on two machines for days.
#
# The read loop is fed by a here-doc, NOT a pipe: a pipeline would run it in a
# subshell and silently swallow the failure count, which is the same class of
# bug this whole command exists to stop.
# THE DARKNESS A RUNG IMPLIES, exported to every hook so nobody restates it.
#
# The mapping lives in hooklib.sh for the shipped plugins, but an INTEGRATOR
# hook needs the identical table and getting it wrong is invisible. tackup's
# kbd-rgb hand-rolled it, read `resume` as "coming back up, so light it", and
# put a lit keyboard over a dark screen -- in the same pass as ddc-monitor,
# which reads the real mapping and was powering the monitor down. Both hooks
# "succeeded".
#
# So the runner states it once. A hook that honours VIGILANCE_INTENT cannot fall
# through a stale `*)` case when the ladder grows a rung, and an integrator no
# longer has to locate a libexec path it does not own just to ask "dark or lit?"
_intent_of() {   # edge -> dark | lit | none
  # DERIVED, because intent belongs to the RUNG an edge lands on. `resume` is
  # dark and `wake` is lit for no reason except where each arrives, so stating
  # it per rung removes every special case that used to be listed here.
  if [ -z "${1:-}" ]; then printf 'none\n'; return 0; fi
  case " $INTENT_MAP " in
    *" $1="*) _io=${INTENT_MAP#*" $1="}
              # guard the FIRST entry, where there is no leading space
              case "$INTENT_MAP" in "$1="*) _io=${INTENT_MAP#"$1="} ;; esac
              printf '%s\n' "${_io%% *}" ;;
    *)        printf 'none\n' ;;
  esac
}

# PATH FOR HOOKS, normalised because inheriting the caller's is a silent trap.
#
# vigilant runs from a keybind, a --user unit, a SYSTEM sleep unit and a greeter
# launcher, and those have four different PATHs. The system manager's is
# /usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin -- no ~/.local/bin -- so a
# hook calling a user-installed command BY NAME finds nothing and, if it does
# not check, reports success. That is exactly how tackup's kbd-rgb no-opped on
# the suspend path while logging a clean crossing.
#
# The user bindir goes FIRST because that is where an integrator's own tools
# live, and the pkg installs vigilance's there too. A hook may still override
# PATH for itself; this only guarantees a sane floor rather than a lottery.
HOOK_PATH=${VIGILANCE_HOOK_PATH:-${XDG_BIN_HOME:-$HOME/.local/bin}:$HOME/bin:\
/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin}

# A HOOK MAY NOT HANG FOREVER, because hooks run in LEXICAL ORDER and the lock
# provider is not first. Measured before this existed: a hook in lock.d that
# slept blocked the traversal indefinitely and `50-provider` never ran -- the
# screen was never locked, with nothing in the log, no alert and no timeout.
#
# Under lock-on-sleep.service that IS the whole failure. The unit is a system
# oneshot with TimeoutStartSec=25; systemd kills it at 25s and NOTHING CAN VETO
# A SUSPEND, so logind proceeds and the box sleeps UNLOCKED. One hung plugin,
# ordered before the provider, silently removes the guarantee this package
# exists to make.
#
# The default is generous on purpose: this bounds a HANG, it does not enforce an
# SLA. A healthy ddcutil pass over three displays is a few seconds, and killing
# a slow-but-working hook would trade a rare hang for a routine failure. It has
# to leave room for the rest of the edge inside the unit's budget.
#
# SAFE FOR THE LOCKER, which looks like the obvious hazard and is not. The
# shipped provider starts swaylock with `systemd-run --user` and returns at
# commit, so the locker is a SIBLING unit no timeout here can reach. Killing a
# foreground locker mid-lock would leave a frozen abandoned ext-session-lock,
# which is precisely why the architecture forbids running one as a child -- so
# this enforces that rule rather than fighting it.
#
# VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT=0 disables it, for debugging a hook interactively.
# "I COULDN'T LOOK" IS NOT "I LOOKED AND IT'S FINE", and conflating them is the
# single shape behind almost every defect this suite has found.
#
# Exit 0 used to mean BOTH "I did the work" and "not applicable here", so
# nothing downstream could tell them apart. Measured consequence: on a desktop
# whose monitor has no DPMS standby and no sysfs backlight, `verify sleep` said
# OK while BOTH of its screen checkers had quietly declined -- ddc-monitor
# because the panel advertises no off code, panel-backlight because there is no
# such device. The screen was verified by nothing and the tier whose whole job
# is to catch that reported green.
#
# So a hook may now say NOT APPLICABLE with exit 78, and the runner counts it
# apart from both success and failure. 78 is sysexits' EX_CONFIG: unusual
# enough not to collide with a real failure, and it already means "this is not
# configured for me".
#
# BACKWARD COMPATIBLE BY CONSTRUCTION: a hook that does not use it exits 0 and
# is counted as having CHECKED, exactly as before. The signal only ever adds
# information; it never turns a working integration into a failing one.
HOOK_NA=78

HOOK_TIMEOUT=${VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT:-10}
HOOK_TMO=
if [ "$HOOK_TIMEOUT" != 0 ] && command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  # -k, or the bound is advisory: a hook ignoring SIGTERM must still die.
  HOOK_TMO="timeout -k 2 $HOOK_TIMEOUT"
fi

# The AUDIT tier gets its own, larger budget. It reads external event sources --
# the shipped one runs journalctl over a day of records -- and a big journal
# legitimately takes longer than anything on the lock path may. Nothing waits on
# it (a daily timer), so latency is not what needs protecting here; only the
# hang does.
AUDIT_TIMEOUT=${VIGILANCE_AUDIT_TIMEOUT:-60}
AUDIT_TMO=
if [ "$AUDIT_TIMEOUT" != 0 ] && command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  AUDIT_TMO="timeout -k 2 $AUDIT_TIMEOUT"
fi

_run_hooks() {   # edge from [suffix]
  _edge=$1; _from=$2; _sfx=${3:-}
  _list=$(_hooks_for "$_edge" "$_sfx")
  # Counters are globals so a caller can ask what actually happened rather than
  # only whether anything failed. Reset per call: a stale count would be a
  # claim about a previous edge.
  HOOKS_CHECKED=0
  HOOKS_NA=0
  HOOKS_NA_NAMES=
  if [ -z "$_list" ]; then return 0; fi
  _fails=0
  while IFS= read -r _h; do
    if [ -z "$_h" ]; then continue; fi
    _sd=$STATE_ROOT/$(basename "$_h")
    mkdir -p "$_sd"
    _hrc=0
    # VIGILANCE_KIND lets ONE plugin serve several kinds: ddc-monitor drives
    # the monitor when act, and reads back VCP D6 when verify, sharing its bus
    # discovery instead of duplicating it in a second file. Without this a hook
    # cannot tell "do it" from "check it": the edge is `sleep` either way.
    VIGILANCE_EDGE="$_edge" VIGILANCE_FROM="$_from" \
      VIGILANCE_SOURCE="${VIGILANCE_SOURCE:-}" \
      VIGILANCE_KIND="$(printf '%s' "${_sfx:-.act}" | tr -d .)" \
      VIGILANCE_INTENT="$(_intent_of "$_edge")" \
      PATH="$HOOK_PATH" \
      VIGILANCE_STATE_DIR="$_sd" \
      $HOOK_TMO "$_h" "$_edge" "$_from" || _hrc=$?
    if [ "$_hrc" = "$HOOK_NA" ]; then
      # NOT APPLICABLE. Not a failure -- there is genuinely nothing here for
      # this hook to do -- but emphatically not a success either, and the
      # difference is what `verify` reports.
      HOOKS_NA=$((HOOKS_NA + 1))
      HOOKS_NA_NAMES="$HOOKS_NA_NAMES $(basename "$_h")"
      continue
    fi
    if [ "$_hrc" -eq 0 ]; then
      HOOKS_CHECKED=$((HOOKS_CHECKED + 1))
    fi
    if [ "$_hrc" -ne 0 ]; then
      # 124 is timeout(1) saying it fired; 137 is the -k SIGKILL after a hook
      # that ignored the TERM. Named apart from an ordinary failure because the
      # remedy differs: a hook that HUNG also held up every later hook on the
      # edge, and that consequence is what the reader needs told.
      case "$_hrc" in
        124|137)
          log "HOOK TIMED OUT after ${HOOK_TIMEOUT}s (rc=$_hrc):"\
" $_edge$_sfx $(basename "$_h") -- it held up every later hook on this edge" ;;
        *) log "HOOK FAILED (rc=$_hrc): $_edge$_sfx $(basename "$_h")" ;;
      esac
      _fails=$((_fails + 1))
      # An ACTUATOR or VERIFY failure means the machine may not be in the
      # state we believe: a monitor that did not sleep, a check that did not
      # confirm. That is worth a human's attention. A REPORT failure is not:
      # nobody was told, but the machine is fine, and alerting on it would
      # train the human to ignore alerts.
      case "$_sfx" in
        .report) ;;
        *) _alert hook-failed \
             "$_edge$_sfx hook $(basename "$_h") failed (rc=$_hrc)" ;;
      esac
    fi
  done <<EOF
$_list
EOF
  if [ "$_fails" -gt 0 ]; then return 1; fi
  return 0
}

# go/force: traverse to a target rung, crossing EVERY edge in between.
#
# Declarative by design. The caller says where the machine should BE, not which
# transition to perform, so no caller has to know the ladder's shape or
# sequence its own steps. `force` differs only in that it does not trust the
# recorded depth: it starts from the far end, so every hook needed to reach the
# target runs. That is the drift-recovery path, for when reality and the depth
# file have diverged.
#
# Depth is committed BEFORE the hooks run, and per step. A hook that fails must
# not leave the recorded rung lying about where the machine is, because the
# ascent reads it to decide restore-versus-re-assert.
# `only` is the single-edge form: cross exactly the one edge that ENTERS the
# target, skipping every rung in between. Direction comes from the current
# depth (deeper target -> the descent edge, shallower -> the ascent edge), so
# it stays symmetric and `only` can reach wake/resume as well as the descent
# edges. Like `force` it ignores the ladder's no-skipping rule.
#
# It still COMMITS the depth. A hook that changed hardware without the record
# following is precisely the desync that left a monitor dark for two days, so
# there is no "run the hooks but pretend nothing happened" mode.
# ONE EDGE, crossed properly: ask, commit, act, announce -- in that order and in
# ONE place. `only` and the traversal loop both need this exact sequence and
# each carried its own copy: two places to get a safety-critical ORDER right.
#
# The order IS the contract:
#   1. BLOCK is asked first, before anything is committed, so a refusal changes
#      nothing at all (exit 3 means the machine did not move).
#   2. DEPTH is committed before the actuators run, so a hook that fails cannot
#      leave the record lying about where the machine is -- the ascent reads
#      that record to decide restore-versus-re-assert.
#   3. REPORT runs after the actuators, so a reporter announces what is now TRUE
#      rather than what was intended.
#
# Returns the command contract's own codes (3 blocked, 1 a hook failed, 0 ok) so
# a caller just propagates rather than re-deriving them.
_cross_one() {   # edge leaving to [note]
  # THE BLOCK TIER MAY REFUSE TO TAKE THE MACHINE DOWN. IT MAY NEVER REFUSE TO
  # BRING IT BACK UP.
  #
  # Refusing a DESCENT is the tier's whole purpose and is conservative in the
  # safe direction: the machine stays where it is, awake and usable, and a
  # debounce or an inhibitor is the mechanism behaving correctly.
  #
  # Refusing an ASCENT is the opposite in every respect. It leaves the machine
  # in a DARK rung with the one path out of it vetoed -- "the screen is dark and
  # nothing will bring it back", stated as a supported configuration. Measured,
  # before this guard: a block hook in wake.block.d made `force open` AND
  # `vigilant rescue` return 3 with the machine still at `sleep`. The panic key
  # is the last resort, pressed blind by someone who cannot see the screen, and
  # a plugin could disable it.
  #
  # Nor is anything lost. This edge does not perform authentication -- swaylock
  # does -- so blocking `unlock` never kept anyone out; it only stopped the
  # RECORD and the restore hooks, desyncing the file from a machine the user has
  # already unlocked. An ascent block is all cost and no benefit, so it is
  # refused here and reported as a wiring error rather than honoured.
  if _is_descent "$1" && _blocked "$1" "$2"; then
    _log "refused $1: $2 (blocked)"
    return 3
  fi
  # DELIBERATELY TOLERANT. A function returning non-zero aborts its caller
  # under set -e, and aborting here would mean a failure to WRITE A FILE
  # suppressed the LOCK. Suppressing a lock is a security failure; a wrong
  # record is only a reporting one, and _set_depth already logs it.
  _set_depth "$3" || true
  _log "cross $1: $2 -> $3${4:+ ($4)}"
  # An ASCENT is the most recent evidence of activity vigilant can have, and it
  # is the one evidence an idle clock can never be blind to. See _idle_secs.
  if ! _is_descent "$1"; then _note_ascent; fi
  # From here until the act tier finishes, the record is ahead of the machine
  # ON PURPOSE. Anything judging the two against each other must skip this
  # window rather than report the gap it is guaranteed to find.
  _crossing_begin
  _crc=0
  _run_hooks "$1" "$2" || _crc=1
  # A reporting failure is loud in the log but does NOT change the exit status:
  # the non-zero codes mean "the machine is not where you asked", and failing to
  # tell logind is not that. A unit must not restart or alarm because a bar was
  # busy.
  _run_hooks "$1" "$2" .report || true
  _crossing_end
  return "$_crc"
}

cmd_go() {   # <state> [force|only|atleast]
  _to=$1
  _mode=${2:-}
  _forced=
  case "$_mode" in force) _forced=1 ;; esac
  if ! _dto=$(_depth_of "$_to"); then
    log "unknown state '$_to' (want: $LADDER)"
    return 2
  fi
  mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR" "$STATE_ROOT"

  # A SECURITY REQUEST MAY DEEPEN THE MACHINE, NEVER RAISE IT.
  #
  # `go <rung>` means "be AT this rung" and correctly travels in either
  # direction: the resume path relies on that, since `go lock` from `sleep` is
  # how a woken box comes back up. But a LOCK REQUEST is a different question
  # wearing the same verb -- it asks to be AT LEAST this deep -- and answering
  # it by ascending turns the screen ON in response to the single most
  # unambiguous "I am leaving" gesture there is.
  #
  # MEASURED ON A LIVE BOX, twice in one evening. Closing the lid on AC makes
  # logind emit Session.Lock; the trigger ran plain `go lock`; the machine was
  # at `sleep`, so it crossed `wake` and lit the panel. Nothing brought it back
  # down, because swayidle had already spent its `timeout 600` and never saw a
  # resume -- a lid switch is not seat input to the compositor. The box sat lit
  # with the lid shut for 30 minutes, and the only thing that ended it was a
  # human opening the lid again.
  #
  # DEEPER ALREADY IMPLIES LOCKED, so declining is a real answer and not a
  # dodge: every route to `sleep` or below crosses the `lock` edge on the way,
  # and the standing recheck re-asserts that every minute. It is LOGGED rather
  # than silent, because a security request that was declined is exactly the
  # kind of thing that must not vanish.
  if [ "$_mode" = atleast ]; then
    _dnow=$(_depth_of "$(_depth)") || _dnow=0
    if [ "$_dnow" -ge "$_dto" ]; then
      # The audit tier reconciles on this line, and it names BOTH rungs so the
      # match can anchor on the ASSERTED one: "already at 'sleep'" alone cannot
      # satisfy an assertion about `lock` without teaching awk the rank order.
      log "already at '$(_depth)', deeper than '$_to'; not raising"
      return 0
    fi
    _mode=
  fi

  if [ "$_mode" = only ]; then
    _dfrom=$(_depth_of "$(_depth)") || _dfrom=0
    if [ "$_dfrom" -eq "$_dto" ]; then
      log "already at '$_to'; nothing to do"
      return 0
    fi
    # One step, in the direction of the target, regardless of distance.
    if [ "$_dfrom" -lt "$_dto" ]; then _edge=$(_edge_down "$_dto")
    else _edge=$(_edge_up "$((_dto + 1))"); fi
    _cross_one "$_edge" "$(_depth)" "$_to" only
    return $?
  fi

  if [ -n "$_forced" ]; then
    # Assume the far end, so every edge toward the target is crossed. For a
    # MIDDLE rung this ascends into it (force sleep fires `resume`), which is
    # the right edge for recovery: resume's whole job is to re-assert dark
    # WITHOUT trusting saved state. The cost is that forcing into a middle rung
    # can flicker (re-assert dark, then light up). Acceptable, because force is
    # the rare drift-recovery path; the display keybind uses `go lock`, an
    # ordinary ascent, and never flickers.
    _dfrom=3
    if [ "$_dto" -ge 3 ]; then _dfrom=0; fi
  else
    _dfrom=$(_depth_of "$(_depth)") || _dfrom=0
  fi

  if [ "$_dfrom" -eq "$_dto" ]; then
    log "already at '$_to'; nothing to do"
    return 0
  fi

  _rc=0
  _i=$_dfrom
  while [ "$_i" -ne "$_dto" ]; do
    _leaving=$(_rung_at "$_i")
    if [ "$_i" -lt "$_dto" ]; then
      _i=$((_i + 1)); _edge=$(_edge_down "$_i")
    else
      _edge=$(_edge_up "$_i"); _i=$((_i - 1))
    fi
    # A BLOCK ABORTS THE WHOLE TRAVERSAL, not just this step. Carrying on to the
    # next rung would skip the refused edge's hooks while still committing its
    # depth, leaving the record claiming a rung whose actuators never ran.
    _src=0
    _cross_one "$_edge" "$_leaving" "$(_rung_at "$_i")" \
      "${_forced:+forced}" || _src=$?
    if [ "$_src" = 3 ]; then return 3; fi
    if [ "$_src" != 0 ]; then _rc=1; fi
  done
  return "$_rc"
}

# --- plan: what WILL run, as whom, in what order ---------------------------
# `hooks` answers "what is wired". It answers it for THIS session, implicitly,
# and in one fixed order. Those two silences hide the faults that have cost the
# most here, and this verb exists to break all three:
#
#   SCOPE. A hook wired in USER scope does not exist for the greeter at all.
#   The greeter's sleep edge darkened the keyboard and left the screen lit
#   because the only blanking hook was user-scope, and nothing ever said so.
#
#   ORDER. It REVERSES on an ascent (machine unwinds first, then user), so one
#   listed order is wrong for half the edges.
#
#   REACHABILITY. _hooks_in lists a hook only `if [ -x ]`, and that test is
#   FALSE for a uid that cannot traverse to the target. A machine-scope hook
#   pointing into a 0750 home therefore does not FAIL for the greeter, it does
#   not EXIST: no log, no alert, no missing-hook warning, and the greeter's
#   hook set is quietly smaller than the wiring declares.
#
# That last one is a defect, not a listing, so it sets a non-zero exit. The
# verb is offline and actuates nothing.
GREETER_USER=${VIGILANCE_GREETER_USER:-_greetd}
PLAN_WHY=""

_plan_bit() {   # path " groups " -> 0 if the greeter has execute on it
  _pb=$(stat -c '%a %U %G' "$1" 2>/dev/null) || return 0
  _pbm=${_pb%% *}; _pbr=${_pb#* }
  _pbu=${_pbr%% *}; _pbg=${_pbr##* }
  case "$_pbm" in *[1357]) return 0 ;; esac    # other-x settles it outright
  if [ "$_pbu" = "$GREETER_USER" ]; then return 0; fi
  case "$2" in *" $_pbg "*) return 0 ;; esac
  # An ACL can grant traversal where the mode bits deny it, and a named entry
  # for the greeter is exactly how one would grant it deliberately. Reading
  # the mode alone would report a correct setup as broken.
  if command -v getfacl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    if getfacl -p "$1" 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^user:$GREETER_USER:..x"
    then return 0; fi
  fi
  return 1
}

_plan_reach() {   # path -> 0 greeter can execute it, 1 no, 2 cannot tell
  if ! id -u "$GREETER_USER" >/dev/null 2>&1; then return 2; fi
  _pr_t=$(readlink -f "$1" 2>/dev/null) || return 2
  if [ -z "$_pr_t" ]; then return 2; fi
  _pr_g=" $(id -nG "$GREETER_USER" 2>/dev/null) "
  # EVERY COMPONENT, not just the target: one non-traversable directory hides
  # what is under it exactly as completely as a non-executable file does, and
  # that is the real shape of the fault (a 0750 home, three levels up).
  _pr_p=""; _pr_rest=${_pr_t#/}
  while [ -n "$_pr_rest" ]; do
    _pr_c=${_pr_rest%%/*}
    case "$_pr_rest" in */*) _pr_rest=${_pr_rest#*/} ;; *) _pr_rest="" ;; esac
    _pr_p="$_pr_p/$_pr_c"
    if ! _plan_bit "$_pr_p" "$_pr_g"; then PLAN_WHY=$_pr_p; return 1; fi
  done
  return 0
}

_plan_names() {   # root edge suffix dir [mark] -> " n1 n2!", space-led
  _pn=$(_hooks_in "$1" "$2" "$3" "$4")
  if [ -z "$_pn" ]; then return 0; fi
  _pn_ifs=$IFS; IFS='
'
  for _pnh in $_pn; do
    IFS=$_pn_ifs
    if [ -z "$_pnh" ]; then continue; fi
    if [ -n "${5:-}" ] && ! _plan_reach "$_pnh"; then
      printf ' %s!' "$(basename "$_pnh")"
    else
      printf ' %s' "$(basename "$_pnh")"
    fi
    IFS='
'
  done
  IFS=$_pn_ifs
}

# 18 chars: "  " + a 7-wide label + " " + "session:". Every row below shares
# it, so a wrapped continuation lines up under the first hook name.
PLAN_IND="                  "

_plan_wrap() {   # prefix " n1 n2 n3"
  _pw_l=$1
  for _pw_n in $2; do
    if [ $(( ${#_pw_l} + 1 + ${#_pw_n} )) -gt 79 ]; then
      printf '%s\n' "$_pw_l"; _pw_l=$PLAN_IND
    fi
    _pw_l="$_pw_l $_pw_n"
  done
  printf '%s\n' "$_pw_l"
}

_plan_row() {   # label who names
  _plan_wrap "$(printf '  %-7s %s' "$1" "$2")" "$3"
}

_plan_edge() {   # edge
  _pe=$1
  if _is_ascent "$_pe"; then _pdir=desc; _pasc=ascent
  else _pdir=asc; _pasc=descent; fi
  printf '\n%s  (%s, intent=%s)\n' "$_pe" "$_pasc" "$(_intent_of "$_pe")"
  for _pt in "" .verify .report .block .due; do
    _pu=$(_plan_names "$HOOK_ROOT" "$_pe" "$_pt" "$_pdir")
    _pm=$(_plan_names "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$_pe" "$_pt" "$_pdir" mark)
    if [ -z "$_pu$_pm" ]; then continue; fi
    _plabel=${_pt#.}; if [ -z "$_plabel" ]; then _plabel=act; fi
    # ORDER MATTERS AND IT FLIPS. Descending, the user layer is torn down
    # first and the machine left for last; coming up, the machine is restored
    # first so a user hook never addresses a device still powered off.
    if _is_ascent "$_pe"; then _pfirst=$_pm; _psecond=$_pu
    else _pfirst=$_pu; _psecond=$_pm; fi
    _plan_row "$_plabel" "session:" "$_pfirst$_psecond"
    # THE GREETER LINE IS THE POINT. It has no user scope at all, so every
    # user-scope hook is simply absent there -- invisible in any listing that
    # shows one merged set, which is how a greeter with no way to blank its
    # screen looked identical to a session that had one.
    _plan_row "" "greeter:" "${_pm:- (nothing)}"
    if [ -n "$_pu" ]; then _plan_row "" "absent :" "$_pu"; fi
  done
}

_plan_gaps() {   # -> 0 clean, 1 a wired machine hook is invisible to greeter
  _pg=0
  for _pge in $(_all_edges) $CROSS_KINDS; do
    for _pgt in "" .verify .report .block .due; do
      case "$_pge" in
        audit|alert|idle|watchdog)
          if [ -n "$_pgt" ]; then continue; fi ;;
      esac
      _pgl=$(_hooks_in "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$_pge" "$_pgt" asc)
      if [ -z "$_pgl" ]; then continue; fi
      _pg_ifs=$IFS; IFS='
'
      for _pgh in $_pgl; do
        IFS=$_pg_ifs
        _pgr=0; _plan_reach "$_pgh" || _pgr=$?
        if [ "$_pgr" = 1 ]; then
          if [ "$_pg" = 0 ]; then
            printf '\nUNREACHABLE BY THE GREETER (wired, but it cannot look)\n'
            _pg=1
          fi
          printf '  %s%s.d/%s\n    -> %s\n    blocked at %s\n' \
            "$_pge" "$_pgt" "$(basename "$_pgh")" \
            "$(readlink -f "$_pgh" 2>/dev/null)" "$PLAN_WHY"
        fi
        IFS='
'
      done
      IFS=$_pg_ifs
    done
  done
  if [ "$_pg" = 1 ]; then
    echo ""
    echo "  These are listed above as if the greeter runs them. It does not:"
    echo "  _hooks_in tests [ -x ], which is FALSE for a uid that cannot"
    echo "  traverse there, so the hook does not fail -- it does not exist."
    echo "  A machine-scope target must live in a root-visible tree."
  fi
  return "$_pg"
}

# A WIRING FINGERPRINT, so two boxes can be compared rather than eyeballed.
#
# Boxes diverging silently is a documented failure here, not a hypothesis: the
# shared rules file ran at 124 lines on one machine and 145 on the other,
# undetected, with the missing block including a hard rule. Wiring is more
# exposed than that, because it is assembled per box by an integrator and
# nothing ever compared the results.
#
# CANONICAL, so equality means something: sorted, and built from scope + edge +
# tier + hook name + the RESOLVED target. The target is included deliberately
# -- two boxes agreeing on the name of a hook that points at different code is
# precisely the drift worth catching, and it is the shape the shadowed
# /usr/local binaries took.
#
# The hash says THAT they differ; `vigilant plan` diffs between boxes to say
# HOW, which is the half a checksum cannot give you.
_plan_fingerprint() {
  { for _fe in $(_all_edges) $CROSS_KINDS; do
      for _ft in "" .verify .report .block .due; do
        case "$_fe" in
          audit|alert) if [ -n "$_ft" ]; then continue; fi ;;
        esac
        for _fs in user machine; do
          if [ "$_fs" = user ]; then _fr=$HOOK_ROOT
          else _fr=$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT; fi
          _fl=$(_hooks_in "$_fr" "$_fe" "$_ft" asc)
          if [ -z "$_fl" ]; then continue; fi
          _fifs=$IFS; IFS='
'
          for _fh in $_fl; do
            IFS=$_fifs
            printf '%s %s%s %s -> %s\n' "$_fs" "$_fe" "$_ft" \
              "$(basename "$_fh")" "$(readlink -f "$_fh" 2>/dev/null)"
            IFS='
'
          done
          IFS=$_fifs
        done
      done
    done; } | sort | cksum | tr -d ' '
}

cmd_plan() {   # [edge]
  echo "== plan: what runs, as whom, in what order =="
  echo ""
  echo "  Nothing below is executed. This is what WOULD run, and as whom."
  echo ""
  printf '  session  uid %s\n             %s\n             + %s\n' \
    "$(id -un 2>/dev/null || echo '?')" "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$HOOK_ROOT"
  if id -u "$GREETER_USER" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '  greeter  uid %s, starts at rung %s\n             %s ONLY\n' \
      "$GREETER_USER" "${VIGILANCE_INITIAL_DEPTH:-lock}" "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT"
  else
    printf '  greeter  no %s account here; reachability NOT checked\n' \
      "$GREETER_USER"
  fi
  echo "             a trailing ! means the greeter cannot reach it"
  for _pe in ${1:-$(_all_edges)}; do
    _plan_edge "$_pe"
  done
  # The cross-cutting tiers are not per-edge. Listing them under one would
  # imply they are keyed by it, which is the exact confusion that let an audit
  # hook in <edge>.audit.d read as wired while nothing would ever run it.
  printf '\ncross-cutting (not per-edge)\n'
  for _pt in $CROSS_KINDS; do
    _pu=$(_plan_names "$HOOK_ROOT" "$_pt" "" asc)
    _pm=$(_plan_names "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$_pt" "" asc mark)
    _pboth=$_pm$_pu
    _plan_row "$_pt" "session:" "${_pboth:- (nothing)}"
    _plan_row "" "greeter:" "${_pm:- (nothing)}"
  done
  # Only for a FULL plan: a fingerprint over the edges you happened to ask
  # about would compare unequal for two boxes that agree entirely.
  if [ -z "${1:-}" ]; then
    printf '\nwiring fingerprint: %s\n' "$(_plan_fingerprint)"
    echo "  identical on two boxes means identical wiring, targets included."
    echo "  if they differ, diff the output of 'vigilant plan' to see how."
  fi
  _plan_gaps || return 1
  return 0
}

cmd_status() {
  _at=$(_depth_at)
  if [ "$_at" -gt 0 ]; then _since="$(( $(date +%s) - _at ))s ago"
  else _since="unknown"; fi
  echo "depth: $(_depth)"
  echo "since: $_since"
  echo "hooks: $HOOK_ROOT (user)"
  echo "       $MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT (machine)"
  echo "state: $RUN_DIR"
}

# An INSPECTABLE config is the antidote to silent misconfiguration, which is
# the class every failure in this cycle belonged to. Every KIND is listed, so
# a missing reporter or a mis-parked due hook is visible without guessing.
# THE PER-EDGE KINDS. `.audit` is deliberately NOT here, and neither is alert:
# both are CROSS-CUTTING and live at the top of a scope, not under an edge. See
# _hooks_cross below for why, and for the bug that listing `.audit` here caused.
KINDS=". .verify .report .due .block"

# CROSS-CUTTING TIERS, which were invisible to this command entirely.
#
# `audit.d`, `alert.d` and `idle.d` are not keyed by edge. An audit hook NAMES
# the edge in its output (one journal source reports events for several
# different edges), an alert is not about a single edge at all, and idle time
# is a property of the SEAT rather than of any edge -- several deadlines on
# different edges all read the same clock.
#
# THE BUG THIS FIXES WAS IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AT ONCE. `.audit` was in KINDS, so
# `hooks` looked in `<edge>.audit.d` and cheerfully reported a hook there as
# wired -- while cmd_audit reads plain `audit.d` and would never run it. And the
# real, working `audit.d` hook on the live box was reported by `hooks` as not
# existing. So the inspector and the runner disagreed about where this tier
# lives, in the one command whose entire purpose is showing what is wired, which
# is the worst possible place for an asserted-versus-actual split.
#
# Listing them under every edge would be a lie; listing them nowhere hid two
# whole tiers. So they get their own section.
_hooks_cross() {
  for _ck in $CROSS_KINDS; do
    echo "$_ck.d:"
    _cl=$(_hooks_for "$_ck")
    if [ -z "$_cl" ]; then echo "  (none)"; continue; fi
    printf '%s\n' "$_cl" | while IFS= read -r _ch; do
      if [ -z "$_ch" ]; then continue; fi
      case "$_ch" in
        "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT"/*) _cs=machine ;;
        *) _cs=user ;;
      esac
      echo "  $(basename "$_ch") [$_cs] -> $_ch"
    done
  done
}
# WIRED BUT WILL NOT RUN, which _hooks_in cannot express. That function lists a
# hook only `if [ -x "$_hh" ]`, the right filter for RUNNING and a terrible one
# for REPORTING: an entry present and not executable becomes indistinguishable
# from one that was never wired at all.
#
# Three faults land in that blind spot, and every one of them is silent:
#
#   unreachable target  a MACHINE-scope hook symlinked into a user's home. The
#                       home is 0750 with other:---, so for the GREETER's uid
#                       `[ -x ]` is simply false and six wired kbd-rgb hooks do
#                       not EXIST from its side. No log, no alert, no warning.
#                       Measured on this box 2026-09-14.
#   dangling symlink    the target moved, or a sweep removed it.
#   forgotten chmod     an integrator's own hook, wired and never made +x.
#
# All three mean "declared, and it will not fire", which is precisely the class
# this suite exists to surface. So they are NAMED rather than filtered away.
_hooks_blocked() {   # <root> <edge> <suffix> -> entries present but unrunnable
  _bd=$1/$2$3.d
  if [ ! -d "$_bd" ]; then return 0; fi
  for _bh in "$_bd"/*; do
    # An unmatched glob yields the pattern itself, which fails -e. A DANGLING
    # symlink fails -e too, so -L is asked as well: otherwise the commonest
    # fault here would be the one this missed.
    if [ ! -e "$_bh" ] && [ ! -L "$_bh" ]; then continue; fi
    # `if`, not `[ -x ] && continue`: under set -e an AND-list whose test FAILS
    # returns non-zero and would abort the caller mid-listing.
    if [ -x "$_bh" ]; then continue; fi
    printf '%s\n' "$_bh"
  done
  return 0
}

# Every blocked entry, across both scopes, all edges and kinds, including the
# cross-cutting tiers. cmd_hooks marks them inline; report counts them.
_blocked_all() {
  for _ba_e in lock sleep suspend unlock wake resume; do
    for _ba_k in $KINDS; do
      case "$_ba_k" in .) _ba_s= ;; *) _ba_s=$_ba_k ;; esac
      _hooks_blocked "$HOOK_ROOT" "$_ba_e" "$_ba_s"
      _hooks_blocked "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$_ba_e" "$_ba_s"
    done
  done
  for _ba_c in audit alert; do
    _hooks_blocked "$HOOK_ROOT" "$_ba_c" ""
    _hooks_blocked "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$_ba_c" ""
  done
  return 0
}

cmd_hooks() {   # [edge]
  for _e in ${1:-lock sleep suspend unlock wake resume}; do
    echo "$_e:"
    _any=0
    for _k in $KINDS; do
      case "$_k" in .) _sfx= ; _lbl= ;; *) _sfx=$_k; _lbl="[${_k#.}] " ;; esac
      _l=$(_hooks_for "$_e" "$_sfx")
      _bl=$(_hooks_blocked "$HOOK_ROOT" "$_e" "$_sfx"
            _hooks_blocked "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT" "$_e" "$_sfx")
      if [ -z "$_l" ] && [ -z "$_bl" ]; then continue; fi
      _any=1
      printf '%s\n' "$_l" | while IFS= read -r _h; do
        if [ -z "$_h" ]; then continue; fi
        case "$_h" in
          "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT"/*) _scope=machine ;;
          *) _scope=user ;;
        esac
        echo "  $_lbl$(basename "$_h") [$_scope] -> $_h"
      done
      # BLOCKED entries are listed too, and marked. Omitting them is what made
      # "wired but unrunnable" read identically to "not wired at all".
      printf '%s\n' "$_bl" | while IFS= read -r _h; do
        if [ -z "$_h" ]; then continue; fi
        case "$_h" in
          "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT"/*) _scope=machine ;;
          *) _scope=user ;;
        esac
        echo "  $_lbl$(basename "$_h") [$_scope] BLOCKED -> $_h (present but"\
" not executable from this session; it will NOT run)"
      done
    done
    if [ "$_any" = 0 ]; then echo "  (none)"; fi
  done
  # Only when listing EVERYTHING. `hooks lock` asks about one edge, and the
  # cross-cutting tiers are not that edge's wiring.
  if [ -z "${1:-}" ]; then _hooks_cross; fi
}

# verify: the REVERSE hooks. For every hook that acts, an optional paired hook
# asserting it took effect. This is the SHARED ORACLE: the test suite and the
# production watchdog call the same predicate, so they cannot drift apart about
# what "correct" means. A verify hook written for one improves both.
# THE SHARED ORACLE. Also the place a false green is most dangerous: an empty
# verify tier used to return 0, so "verify clean" meant "nothing was asked" and
# read exactly like "everything checked out". That is the failure this project
# exists to catch, reproduced inside the thing built to catch it.
#
# So an empty tier reports n/a EXPLICITLY. Exit stays 0 (a box with no verify
# hooks is not broken, and a timer must not alarm), but no caller can mistake
# it for a pass.
# The edge whose intent describes a rung. Verifying is asking "does the
# hardware match the rung we believe we are at", so the relevant assertion is
# the one belonging to the state we are IN.
#
#   open -> unlock (lit)   lock -> lock (lit)
#   sleep -> sleep (dark)  suspend -> suspend (dark)
_verify_edge_for() {   # rung -> the edge whose verify tier asks about it
  # The DESCENT edge into the rung, or the ascent edge where nothing descends
  # into it -- which is only `open`, the top of the ladder.
  _lt | awk -v r="$1" '$1 == r { print ($2 == "-" ? $3 : $2); exit }'
}

cmd_verify() {   # [edge]
  mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR" "$STATE_ROOT"
  _from=$(_depth)
  _vrc=0
  _ran=0
  # Default to the CURRENT rung, not every edge. Checking them all asserts
  # mutually exclusive things at once: at `open` it demanded the monitor be
  # BOTH lit (unlock) and dark (sleep), so a healthy awake machine reported
  # drift and raised an alert. A verifier that cries wolf on a correct machine
  # is worse than none, because it trains you to ignore the real one.
  for _e in ${1:-$(_verify_edge_for "$_from")}; do
    if [ -n "$(_hooks_for "$_e" .verify)" ]; then
      _ran=1
      if _run_hooks "$_e" "$_from" .verify; then
        # SAY WHAT WAS ACTUALLY CHECKED. "ok" alone answers a question nobody
        # asked: a tier where every hook DECLINED reads identically to one
        # where every hook CONFIRMED. Naming the n/a hooks is what makes
        # "nothing looked at the screen" visible at a glance.
        if [ "${HOOKS_CHECKED:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
          # NOTHING COULD BE CHECKED, so this is not a verdict about the
          # machine at all -- it is the absence of one, and it must not read as
          # success. Exactly the state a desktop with no DPMS standby and no
          # sysfs backlight was in while `verify sleep` said OK and a lit
          # wallpaper sat on an OLED.
          echo "verify $_e: NOTHING CHECKED -- ${HOOKS_NA:-0} hook(s) all"\
" declined (${HOOKS_NA_NAMES# }). This edge has no working verifier, so its"\
" state is unknown rather than good"
          _vrc=1
        elif [ "${HOOKS_NA:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
          echo "verify $_e: ok ($HOOKS_CHECKED checked, $HOOKS_NA n/a:"\
" ${HOOKS_NA_NAMES# })"
        else
          echo "verify $_e: ok ($HOOKS_CHECKED checked)"
        fi
      else
        echo "verify $_e: FAIL"
        _vrc=1
      fi
    fi
  done
  if [ "$_ran" = 0 ]; then
    # NAME the edge. "no verify hooks installed" sent vigilance-report to a
    # flat [FAIL] on a machine that had three of them wired -- they were just
    # on other edges than the one this rung asks about. An honest n/a has to
    # say WHICH question went unanswered, or the next layer up mis-reports it.
    echo "verify: n/a (no verify hooks for edge" \
"'${1:-$(_verify_edge_for "$_from")}'; nothing was checked)"
    # 78, NOT 0. "Nothing was checked" must never leave here as success.
    #
    # The n/a contract was closed at the HOOK level (a hook declines with 78)
    # and left open at the EDGE level: every wired hook declining is a FAIL,
    # while NO hook being wired returned 0. Identical epistemic state, opposite
    # verdict -- the precise conflation this whole tier exists to break.
    #
    # It is load-bearing, not tidiness. lock-on-sleep.service runs
    # `vigilant verify lock` as ExecStartPost, and that is the suspend-time
    # lock guarantee. With 0 here, emptying lock.verify.d -- a sweep, a
    # rename, a wiring bug, exactly what hit swayidle-mgr and 50-swaylock --
    # made the unit report success and the box suspend with the lock verified
    # by nothing, green all the way up.
    #
    # DISTINCT FROM 1 on purpose. Both mean no verdict, but the remedies
    # differ: 1 (all declined) says fix the mechanism, 78 says wire one.
    _vrc=$HOOK_NA
  fi
  return "$_vrc"
}

# --- report: does reality match what we believe? ----------------------------
# A DIFFERENT QUESTION from an integrator's `check`, and both are needed. check
# asks "is the wiring canonical" (declared config vs installed config); this
# asks "is the machine doing what the record says" (recorded state vs the
# hardware). Every bug in this project has lived in the second gap, and it is
# the one nobody can see on a headless box.
#
# Absorbed from tackup's vigilance-report, because none of it was tackup's: the
# depth file, the hooks, the units and the ladder are all vigilance's own, and
# a host had been reimplementing knowledge of them from outside.
#
# The marker vocabulary is setup.sh's contract, unchanged: plain [OK]/[FAIL]/
# [WARN]/[--] that an integrator styles.
#
# The sysfs roots are OVERRIDABLE for one reason: otherwise report is untestable
# without the developer's own hardware answering. A scenario that reads
# /sys/class/backlight asserts on whatever the laptop happens to be doing, and
# this suite already learned where that leads -- a test that reached the real
# brightnessctl blacked out a live screen twice. Production never sets these.
SYS_BACKLIGHT=${VIGILANCE_SYS_BACKLIGHT:-/sys/class/backlight}
SYS_DRM=${VIGILANCE_SYS_DRM:-/sys/class/drm}
SYS_LEDS=${VIGILANCE_SYS_LEDS:-/sys/class/leds}

RRC=0
_r_ok()   { printf '  [OK]   %s\n' "$*"; }
_r_bad()  { printf '  [FAIL] %s\n' "$*"; RRC=1; }
_r_warn() { printf '  [WARN] %s\n' "$*"; }
_r_info() { printf '  [--]   %s\n' "$*"; }

# The graphical session, by logind's criteria rather than a pgrep. An ssh
# session is Class=user too, and a box with five of them has no display at all;
# reporting "swayidle NOT running" there is crying wolf, which trains you to
# ignore the line that matters. Session-scoped checks say n/a when absent.
# IS A LOCKER UP? Overridable for the same reason as the sysfs roots: without
# it a scenario's verdict depends on whether the DEVELOPER's screen happens to
# be locked while the suite runs. That is not a hypothetical -- it failed here
# exactly that way, reading a real swaylock from inside a sandbox.
_r_locker_up() {
  if [ -n "${VIGILANCE_LOCKER_UP+x}" ]; then
    [ "$VIGILANCE_LOCKER_UP" = 1 ]
    return $?
  fi
  pgrep -x "${VIGILANCE_LOCKER:-swaylock}" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

_r_session() {
  # Overridable so a scenario can exercise BOTH the session and no-session
  # paths. They behave differently (a fresh session makes the `open` default a
  # real claim; no session makes it a fiction), and without this the test would
  # assert whatever the developer's box happens to be doing -- the same reach
  # into the running system the sysfs roots are overridable to avoid.
  if [ -n "${VIGILANCE_SESSION+x}" ]; then printf '%s' "$VIGILANCE_SESSION"
    return 0
  fi
  loginctl list-sessions --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1}' \
    | while read -r _s; do
        [ -n "$_s" ] || continue
        eval "$(loginctl show-session "$_s" \
          -p Type -p Class -p State -p Seat 2>/dev/null)"
        case "${Type:-}:${Class:-}:${State:-}" in
          wayland:user:active|x11:user:active)
            [ -n "${Seat:-}" ] && { echo "$_s"; break; } ;;
        esac
      done
}

# THE SECTIONS, split out of what had grown to a 177-line cmd_report. Each
# answers ONE question, and they share the facts the first two establish
# ($_sess, $_rung, $_at, $_have_record) through globals rather than re-deriving
# them. That sharing is deliberate: probing the session or the depth in three
# places is how two halves of one report come to disagree with each other, and a
# self-contradicting report is worse than a terse one.
_rep_session() {
  echo "-- session --"
  _sess=$(_r_session)
  if [ -n "$_sess" ]; then _r_ok "graphical session $_sess active"
  else _r_warn "NO graphical session; session-scoped checks report n/a"; fi
}

_rep_state() {
  echo "-- recorded state --"
  # A DEFAULT IS NOT ALWAYS A CLAIM, and treating it as one cries wolf.
  #
  # _depth() answers `open` when no depth file exists. With a live session that
  # is a fair claim -- a fresh login genuinely IS open, and the runtime dir is
  # cleared on logout, so a just-logged-in box legitimately has no record yet.
  # With NO session it is a fiction.
  #
  # Caught on a real machine: nobody logged in, the greeter (a different user,
  # with its own runtime dir and its own depth file) had correctly put the
  # monitor into DDC standby, and this user had no record at all. Report took
  # the default `open`, asserted the screen must therefore be lit, and declared
  # FAIL over a machine doing exactly the right thing.
  #
  # So the claim is real when there is a record OR a session to which the
  # default applies. Otherwise there is nothing of ours to check.
  _at=$(_depth_at)
  _rung=$(_depth)
  _have_record=
  if [ "$_at" -gt 0 ]; then
    _r_info "depth=$_rung ($(( $(date +%s) - _at ))s ago)"
    _have_record=1
  elif [ -n "$_sess" ]; then
    _r_info "depth=$_rung (no record yet; a fresh session starts here)"
    _have_record=1
  else
    _r_info "depth: NO RECORD and no session of ours; asserting nothing about"\
" hardware another session may own"
  fi
  # OUTSTANDING SAVES are the sharpest signal here. A hook saves the level it
  # is about to dim FROM and deletes the file once restored, so one still
  # present at a LIT rung means a descent dimmed something the ascent never put
  # back: "the screen is dark and nothing will bring it back", on disk.
  _saves=
  for _f in "$STATE_ROOT"/*/*; do
    [ -f "$_f" ] || continue
    # A SAVED LEVEL IS A FILE CONTAINING A LEVEL. This scanned every file any
    # hook kept in its state dir, which was the same thing only while the sole
    # state anyone kept WAS a brightness save. An idle source keeps a counter
    # snapshot there permanently, and report called it an unrestored device at
    # every lit rung -- a FAIL, forever, about a file working exactly as meant.
    #
    # Reading the content rather than the name because there is no naming
    # convention to lean on: the savers use `level`, and mute-leds uses the LED
    # name. What they share is the CONTENT, which is the level to restore to.
    case "$(cat "$_f" 2>/dev/null)" in
      ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;;
    esac
    _saves="$_saves $(basename "$(dirname "$_f")")/$(basename "$_f")"
  done
  case "$_rung:$_saves" in
    *:  ) ;;
    open:*|lock:*) _r_bad "saved levels outstanding at a lit rung:$_saves" ;;
    *) _r_info "saved levels held (expected while dark):$_saves" ;;
  esac
}

_rep_hardware() {
  echo "-- hardware --"
  for _b in "$SYS_BACKLIGHT"/*; do
    [ -d "$_b" ] || continue
    _r_info "$(basename "$_b"): actual=$(cat "$_b/actual_brightness" \
2>/dev/null)/$(cat "$_b/max_brightness" 2>/dev/null)" \
"bl_power=$(cat "$_b/bl_power" 2>/dev/null)"
  done
  for _c in "$SYS_DRM"/card*-*; do
    [ -d "$_c" ] || continue
    [ "$(cat "$_c/status" 2>/dev/null)" = connected ] || continue
    _r_info "$(basename "$_c"): dpms=$(cat "$_c/dpms" 2>/dev/null)" \
"enabled=$(cat "$_c/enabled" 2>/dev/null)"
  done

}

# COHERENCE. Deliberately NOT a second DDC implementation: the monitor's real
# power state is ddc-monitor's question, and `verify` asks it through the hook.
# Reimplementing ddcutil here is how the same fact ends up in two
# places that can disagree.
_rep_coherence() {
  echo "-- coherence --"
  if [ -z "$_have_record" ]; then
    # Nothing to be coherent WITH. Both halves of the comparison must be real:
    # without a record there is only the hardware, and hardware alone is not
    # drift. Checking anyway is how a greeter-dimmed monitor read as a fault.
    _r_info "n/a: no depth record to compare the hardware against"
  else
  case "$_rung" in
    sleep|suspend)
      _lit=
      for _b in "$SYS_BACKLIGHT"/*; do
        [ -d "$_b" ] || continue
        _a=$(cat "$_b/actual_brightness" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
        _m=$(cat "$_b/max_brightness" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
        case "$_a$_m" in *[!0-9]*|'') continue ;; esac
        [ "$_m" -gt 1 ] && [ "$_a" -gt $((_m / 10)) ] && _lit=1
      done
      if [ -n "$_lit" ]; then
        _r_bad "depth says '$_rung' but a backlight is still lit"
      else _r_ok "depth '$_rung' matches dark hardware"; fi ;;
    open)
      if _r_locker_up; then
        _r_bad "depth says 'open' but a locker is UP (an edge was missed)"
      else _r_ok "depth 'open' matches an unlocked session"; fi ;;
    lock)
      # A LOCKER IS ONLY EXPECTED WHERE ONE CAN BE RAISED. A GREETER is the
      # locked state: it sits at this rung with no user session, no lock
      # provider and no locker, forever and correctly. Asserting one there made
      # report permanently RED on the one session nobody is present to watch,
      # which is worse than silence -- a check that is always failing is a check
      # nobody reads.
      #
      # The provider is what decides. If no lock.d hook is wired, nothing in
      # this session could produce a locker, so demanding one is asserting
      # something no mechanism here can satisfy. Said out loud rather than
      # skipped, because "no opinion" must not read as "checked and fine".
      if [ -z "$(_hooks_for lock)" ]; then
        _r_info "depth 'lock' with no lock provider wired, so no locker is"\
" expected (a greeter IS the locked state)"
      elif _r_locker_up; then
        _r_ok "depth 'lock' matches a live locker"
      else _r_bad "depth says 'lock' but no locker is running"; fi ;;
    *) _r_warn "unknown rung '$_rung'" ;;
  esac
  fi
}

# VERIFY, for the same reason as coherence: only when there is a claim to
# verify. `verify` asks the hooks to assert the CURRENT rung against the
# hardware, and with no record the "current rung" is a default rather than
# something we believe.
#
# Capture the OUTPUT, not just the status: an empty tier exits 0 and used
# to read here as "clean" when nothing had been asked. A green light
# meaning "nobody checked" is worse than no light.
# EDGES THAT ACT BUT CANNOT BE CHECKED, asked statically for EVERY edge.
#
# `verify` and the standing recheck only ever ask about the rung the machine is
# at NOW, so an edge with no verifier is invisible until the machine happens to
# be there. For `resume` that is after an S3 cycle -- the one moment nobody is
# watching, on the edge with a history of failing.
#
# Found live on both boxes: `resume` fires four actuators and has no verify
# tier at all, and `vigilant verify resume` reported success.
_unverified_edges() {   # -> "edge:actcount" for each acting, uncheckable edge
  for _ue in $(_all_edges); do
    _uea=0; _uev=0
    for _uer in "$HOOK_ROOT" "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT"; do
      for _ueh in "$_uer/$_ue.d"/*; do
        if [ -x "$_ueh" ]; then _uea=$((_uea + 1)); fi
      done
      for _ueh in "$_uer/$_ue.verify.d"/*; do
        if [ -x "$_ueh" ]; then _uev=$((_uev + 1)); fi
      done
    done
    if [ "$_uea" -gt 0 ] && [ "$_uev" = 0 ]; then
      printf '%s:%s\n' "$_ue" "$_uea"
    fi
  done
}

_rep_coverage() {
  # ITS OWN SECTION, so an assertion can be scoped to it. report has one exit
  # code for ten sections and several read the real host, so a test that keys
  # on the whole output passes for free on any host that is red elsewhere --
  # and keeps passing with the check it claims to cover deleted.
  echo "-- coverage --"
  _uo=$(_unverified_edges)
  if [ -z "$_uo" ]; then
    _r_ok "every acting edge has a verify tier wired"
    return 0
  fi
  for _u in $_uo; do
    # WARN, matching the existing "verify: n/a" severity: this is a wiring gap,
    # not a machine fault, and it is actionable and rare rather than always-on.
    _r_warn "edge '${_u%:*}' acts (${_u#*:} hooks) with NO verify tier;" \
      "it drives hardware and asks nobody whether it worked"
  done
  return 0
}

# CAN AN OVERDUE EDGE BE DETECTED AT ALL? "Armed" is not "reachable", which is
# the "enabled is not working" lesson one layer out.
#
# This used to ask whether FORCING could ever act; forcing is retired, so the
# question is now the one that matters: for each deadline, is anything able to
# tell whether it has passed? An idle-anchored deadline needs an idle source
# (idle.d); a rung-anchored one is always measurable. A deadline nobody can
# measure is not a deadline, it is a sentence in a config file.
#
# The live answer before idle.d existed was "none of them", on both boxes.
_rep_deadlines() {
  echo "-- deadlines --"
  _dclock=$(_idle_secs)
  if [ -n "$_dclock" ]; then
    _r_ok "idle clock: ${_dclock}s since last input"
  else
    _r_info "idle clock: none wired (idle.d); idle-anchored deadlines cannot"\
" be measured here"
  fi
  for _dr in $LADDER; do
    _de=$(_next_down "$_dr")
    if [ -z "$_de" ]; then continue; fi
    _dd=$(_due_for "$_de" "$_dr")
    if [ -z "$_dd" ]; then
      _r_info "from '$_dr': '$_de' declares no deadline"
      continue
    fi
    _da=$(printf '%s' "$_dd" | awk '{print $2}')
    _ds=$(printf '%s' "$_dd" | awk '{print $1}')
    if [ "$_da" != idle ]; then
      _r_ok "from '$_dr': '$_de' ${_ds}s $_da-anchored, measurable"
      continue
    fi
    if [ -n "$_dclock" ]; then
      _r_ok "from '$_dr': '$_de' ${_ds}s idle-anchored, measurable"
      continue
    fi
    # THE SLIVER NOTHING ELSE COVERS, said plainly. A wedged idle timer is
    # running, correctly armed and silent: it passes the argv check, logs no
    # event for audit, and leaves the machine at a rung it genuinely matches.
    # Without an idle clock nothing in this suite can see it, and it is the
    # failure this package was created for.
    _r_warn "from '$_dr': '$_de' is ${_ds}s idle-anchored and NOTHING can"\
" measure it; a silently wedged idle timer would go unnoticed"
  done
  return 0
}

_rep_verify() {
  if [ -z "$_have_record" ]; then
    _r_info "verify n/a: nothing was asserted, so there is nothing to check"
  else
    _vout=$(cmd_verify 2>&1) && _vrc2=0 || _vrc2=1
    # FOUR OUTCOMES, and they are not interchangeable. The old matcher looked
    # for the substring "n/a" anywhere, which now also appears in a perfectly
    # healthy "ok (3 checked, 1 n/a: ...)" -- so it has to match the SPECIFIC
    # claims instead of a word that shows up in several of them.
    case "$_vout" in
      *"NOTHING CHECKED"*)
        # Every wired hook declined. Not drift -- there is no verdict at all,
        # which is worse than drift because it reads as calm.
        _r_bad "$_vout" ;;
      *"verify: n/a"*)
        # No verify hooks for this edge: a wiring gap, not a machine fault.
        _r_warn "$_vout" ;;
      *)
        if [ "$_vrc2" = 0 ]; then _r_ok "${_vout:-verify clean}"
        else _r_bad "verify reports drift: $_vout"; fi ;;
    esac
  fi

}

# ENABLED IS NOT WORKING, and conflating the two is the most expensive mistake
# this project has made. lock-on-sleep.service sat `enabled` and `canonical` for
# an entire refactor while its ExecStart named a path that did not exist: `%h`
# in a SYSTEM unit resolves to ROOT's home regardless of User=, so it
# 203/EXEC'd on every single sleep. 175 clean-looking starts, then 5 failures
# nobody saw. The suspend-lock guarantee was fiction, and both properties we
# checked (enabled, canonical) hold for a unit naming a binary that is absent.
#
# So ask the two questions those two miss, both of which are cheap and direct:
# does the named binary EXIST AND RUN, and what happened LAST time it was asked?
_rep_unit_runnable() {   # unit
  _x=$(systemctl show -p ExecStart --value "$1" 2>/dev/null \
    | sed -n 's/.*path=\([^ ;]*\).*/\1/p' | head -1)
  if [ -n "$_x" ] && [ ! -x "$_x" ]; then
    # Named, because "it failed" without the path sends you reading units.
    _r_bad "$1 ExecStart is NOT executable: $_x -- it will 203/EXEC, and a"\
" failed Before=sleep.target unit does not stop the sleep"
  fi
  # The LAST RESULT is the only direct evidence that it ever worked. A unit that
  # has never run at all reports success here, so this is a floor, not a proof.
  _res=$(systemctl show -p Result --value "$1" 2>/dev/null || true)
  case "${_res:-}" in
    ''|success) ;;
    *) _r_bad "$1 last run FAILED (Result=$_res)" ;;
  esac
}

# ARE THE IDLE TIMER'S COMMANDS ACTUALLY RUNNABLE? The same question the system
# units are now asked, for the same reason: a timer armed with a command that
# cannot exec fails SILENTLY, and forever.
#
# NOT HYPOTHETICAL. A sweep removed a stale /usr/local/bin/swayidle-mgr while a
# RUNNING swayidle held that exact path in its argv. The 480s idle-lock stopped
# working from that instant and the machine stopped auto-locking. It left no
# trace: vigilant's log shows nothing (no edge was even attempted), the journal
# shows nothing (swayidle reports a failed exec only under -d), and `verify` is
# content because a box that never locked sits correctly at `open`. The 600s
# `go sleep` timer still worked, so the screen still darkened on idle and
# everything LOOKED right. Found by reading argv by hand, hours later.
#
# READ WHAT THE RUNNING PROCESS IS ARMED WITH, not what the config says it
# should be. Those are different facts and the gap between them is where this
# failure lives: a daemon pins its argv at start, so a file that moves under it
# is invisible to every config-level check ever written.
#
# The argv SOURCE is overridable for the same reason the sysfs roots and the
# locker probe are: otherwise this assertion depends on whatever swayidle the
# DEVELOPER happens to be running, and the test reaches into the live session to
# find it. Production never sets it.
# THE IDLE TIMER IS A MECHANISM, AND report SHOULD NOT KNOW WHICH ONE.
#
# Everything below named `swayidle` outright. The crossing machinery hardcodes
# no mechanism at all -- measured, zero references across all nine of its
# functions -- but the OBSERVABILITY did, and that is the worse half: swap
# swayidle for xidlehook or hypridle and the machine locks perfectly while
# `report` says "swayidle NOT running: nothing will lock on idle". A false FAIL
# about a healthy box, from the one tier whose entire job is not doing that.
#
# `VIGILANCE_IDLE_CMDLINE` looked like the dial for this and was not: it
# substitutes the cmdline SOURCE for tests while the process name stayed fixed.
#
# Set VIGILANCE_IDLE_PROCESS empty to say the box HAS no idle timer, and the
# whole tier reports n/a rather than red -- a greeter-only or kiosk machine has
# nothing to idle.
IDLE_PROCESS=${VIGILANCE_IDLE_PROCESS-swayidle}
IDLE_UNIT=${VIGILANCE_IDLE_UNIT-vigilance-idle.service}

_rep_idle_armed() {
  if [ -n "${VIGILANCE_IDLE_CMDLINE:-}" ]; then
    _icl=$VIGILANCE_IDLE_CMDLINE
  else
    [ -n "$IDLE_PROCESS" ] || return 0
    _ipid=$(pgrep -x "$IDLE_PROCESS" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
    [ -n "${_ipid:-}" ] || return 0
    _icl=/proc/$_ipid/cmdline
  fi
  [ -r "$_icl" ] || return 0
  _iseen=0 _ibad=0 _istate=0
  # swayidle's grammar: `timeout <secs> <cmd>`, and `resume`/`before-sleep`/
  # `after-resume`/`lock`/`unlock` each take a <cmd> directly. A <cmd> is ONE
  # argv element carrying its own arguments, so only its first word is a binary.
  while IFS= read -r _ia; do
    if [ "$_istate" = 1 ]; then _istate=2; continue; fi
    if [ "$_istate" = 2 ]; then
      _istate=0
      _icmd=${_ia%% *}
      [ -n "$_icmd" ] || continue
      _iseen=$((_iseen + 1))
      case "$_icmd" in
        /*) [ -x "$_icmd" ] && continue ;;
        *)  PATH="$HOOK_PATH" command -v "$_icmd" >/dev/null 2>&1 && continue ;;
      esac
      _r_bad "$IDLE_PROCESS is armed with a command that cannot run:"\
" $_icmd -- that"\
" timer fires into nothing, silently, and no other check can see it"
      _ibad=$((_ibad + 1))
      continue
    fi
    case "$_ia" in
      timeout) _istate=1 ;;
      resume|before-sleep|after-resume|lock|unlock) _istate=2 ;;
    esac
  done <<EOF
$(tr '\0' '\n' < "$_icl")
EOF
  if [ "$_iseen" = 0 ]; then
    _r_warn "$IDLE_PROCESS is running with NO timers armed; nothing locks"\
" on idle"
  elif [ "$_ibad" = 0 ]; then
    _r_ok "$_iseen idle timer command(s) armed and runnable"
  fi
}

_rep_machinery() {
  echo "-- machinery --"
  if systemctl --user is-active --quiet vigilance-logind.service 2>/dev/null
  then _r_ok "vigilance-logind.service active"
  elif [ -z "$_sess" ]; then _r_info "vigilance-logind n/a (no session)"
  else _r_bad "vigilance-logind.service NOT active (Session.Lock is deaf)"; fi
  for _u in lock-on-sleep.service vigilance-resume.service; do
    if systemctl is-enabled --quiet "$_u" 2>/dev/null; then
      _r_ok "$_u enabled"
    else _r_bad "$_u NOT enabled"; fi
    _rep_unit_runnable "$_u"
  done
  # WHO SUPERVISES THE SUPERVISOR. These two timers ARE the second pillar, and
  # nothing here reported on them at all.
  #
  # Not hypothetical: the enforce timer was stopped BY HAND on a real box to
  # quiet an alert storm, and then simply stayed stopped, with every subsequent
  # report green. "A supervision loop nothing runs" is the dead tier this
  # project keeps rediscovering -- `due.d` was designed, enumerated and read
  # by nothing for a whole refactor. So its absence is a FAILURE, not silence.
  for _t in vigilance-enforce.timer vigilance-audit.timer; do
    if systemctl --user is-active --quiet "$_t" 2>/dev/null; then
      _r_ok "$_t active"
    elif [ -z "$_sess" ]; then _r_info "$_t n/a (no session)"
    else _r_bad "$_t NOT active: supervision is off, so a missed edge will go"\
" unnoticed -- which is the one thing this suite exists to prevent"; fi
  done
  if [ -z "$IDLE_PROCESS" ]; then
    _r_info "idle timer n/a (VIGILANCE_IDLE_PROCESS is empty: this box"\
" declares none)"
  elif pgrep -x "$IDLE_PROCESS" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _r_ok "$IDLE_PROCESS running (timer)"
  elif [ -z "$_sess" ]; then
    _r_info "$IDLE_PROCESS n/a (no session to idle in)"
  else
    _r_bad "$IDLE_PROCESS NOT running: nothing will lock on idle"
  fi
  # RUNNING IS NOT WORKING, the same distinction as enabled-is-not-working.
  #
  # Called UNCONDITIONALLY, and the function decides for itself whether it has
  # argv to read. Nesting it under the pgrep above made it unreachable whenever
  # swayidle was not running -- which is harmless in production (there is no
  # argv, and a dead swayidle already FAILS on the line above) but made the
  # check untestable in the VM substrate, where no session exists. The stub tier
  # passed only because the DEVELOPER's box happened to have swayidle running,
  # i.e. the test was reading the host after all. The VM caught it.
  _rep_idle_armed
  # The unit that keeps swayidle ALIVE, which is a different claim from swayidle
  # running. A bare swayidle works right up until it wedges, and wedging is the
  # original failure this entire suite was built around: one wedge-prone daemon
  # must not BE the guarantee. Running-but-unsupervised is degraded, not broken,
  # so it warns rather than fails -- a dead timer already failed above.
  if [ -z "$IDLE_UNIT" ]; then
    _r_info "no idle supervisor declared (VIGILANCE_IDLE_UNIT is empty)"
  elif systemctl --user is-active --quiet "$IDLE_UNIT" 2>/dev/null; then
    _r_ok "$IDLE_UNIT active (the idle timer is supervised)"
  elif [ -z "$_sess" ]; then
    _r_info "$IDLE_UNIT n/a (no session)"
  else
    _r_warn "$IDLE_UNIT NOT active: $IDLE_PROCESS is unsupervised, so a wedge"\
" will not be restarted"
  fi

}

# ACTUATOR WRITABILITY. hooklib DECLARES that the login user must be in the
# `vigilant` group for brightnessctl to drive these nodes, and nothing checked
# it, so the requirement was a comment. On a real box the user was in none of
# input/video/i2c and every write was denied -- and hook_dark/hook_lit swallow
# brightnessctl failure by design (`|| true`), so vigilant logged clean
# crossings while the hardware never moved. Asserted-versus-actual drift, in
# the actuators this time rather than the record.
#
# Tests -w on the node rather than writing one: a report must change nothing,
# and a permission bit answers the question without touching the device. A
# node that does not EXIST is n/a, not drift -- a desktop has no panel
# backlight and no hook should care.
# THE EDGE BUDGET: hooks x bound, against the unit's own timeout.
#
# Two numbers in two different files that must relate, and nothing related them.
# lock-on-sleep.service is a system oneshot with TimeoutStartSec=25, covering
# BOTH its ExecStart (`go lock`) and its ExecStartPost (`verify lock`).
#
# THE NUMBER THAT MATTERS IS NOT THE TOTAL, IT IS THE TIME BEFORE THE SCREEN IS
# LOCKED. Hooks run in lexical order and the block tier runs before any of them,
# so every hook ordered ahead of the provider is a hook that can delay the lock
# itself. Measured on a live box: block `10-phantom` and act `20-mute-on-lock`
# both precede `50-swaylock`, so at the 10s default the provider can start at
# 24s of a 25s budget -- systemd then kills the unit mid-lock and logind
# suspends anyway, because nothing can veto a suspend.
#
# The act tier is where the screen is locked, so (block + act) is the critical
# span; report and verify run after and only risk the UNIT being killed once the
# lock has already happened. They are reported separately for that reason.
#
# WORST CASE, not typical. The typical crossing is milliseconds; the only number
# that can reach the timeout is the pathological one.
#
# A WARN, not a FAIL: the machine is working and its headroom is not. A correct
# box turned red is how a report teaches you to stop reading it.
# -> seconds, "" when there is legitimately nothing to check, or "?" when there
# IS a value and this cannot read it. The three are deliberately distinct: an
# absent unit is machinery's finding to report, but a timeout in a form this
# does not understand means the check silently did nothing, which is the failure
# mode the whole project is about. Conflating them hid exactly that.
_rep_budget_secs() {
  _bg=${VIGILANCE_SLEEP_BUDGET:-}
  if [ -z "$_bg" ]; then
    _bg=$(systemctl show lock-on-sleep.service -p TimeoutStartUSec --value \
            2>/dev/null)
  fi
  case "$_bg" in
    ""|infinity) return 0 ;;               # no unit, or no timeout to exceed
    *s)          _bg=${_bg%s} ;;
    *)           printf '?'; return 0 ;;
  esac
  case "$_bg" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*) printf '?'; return 0 ;;   # "1min 30s" and friends
  esac
  printf '%s' "$_bg"
}

_rep_hookcount() {   # <suffix>... -> how many hooks across those tiers
  _hc=0
  for _hcs in "$@"; do
    if [ "$_hcs" = act ]; then _hcs=""; fi
    _hcl=$(_hooks_for lock "$_hcs")
    if [ -n "$_hcl" ]; then
      _hc=$((_hc + $(printf '%s\n' "$_hcl" | grep -c .)))
    fi
  done
  printf '%s' "$_hc"
}

# WHICH act hook raises the locker, by POSITION -- because that, not the total,
# decides whether the screen is locked inside the unit's budget. Hooks ordered
# after the provider can only cost the unit; hooks ordered before it can cost
# the LOCK.
#
# Identified by CONVENTION rather than configuration: a lock provider resolves
# into a `providers/` directory (vigilance ships its own at
# libexec/vigilance/providers/swaylock and an integrator symlinks it into
# lock.d). That keeps the fact in one place -- no second declaration to drift
# from the wiring, which is the failure this project keeps paying for.
#
# UNKNOWN IS PESSIMISTIC. A third-party provider that does not follow the
# convention yields no position, and then every act hook is assumed to precede
# the lock. That is the safe direction, and _rep_budget says so rather than
# quietly presenting a guess as a measurement.
_lock_provider_pos() {   # -> 1-based position among act hooks, or ""
  _pl=$(_hooks_for lock)
  if [ -z "$_pl" ]; then return 0; fi
  _pp=0
  _pfound=
  while IFS= read -r _ph; do
    if [ -z "$_ph" ]; then continue; fi
    _pp=$((_pp + 1))
    case "$(readlink -f "$_ph" 2>/dev/null)" in
      */providers/*) _pfound=$_pp; break ;;
    esac
  done <<EOF
$_pl
EOF
  printf '%s' "$_pfound"
}

_rep_budget() {
  _bsec=$(_rep_budget_secs)
  if [ -z "$_bsec" ]; then return 0; fi   # machinery reports an absent unit
  if [ "$_bsec" = "?" ]; then
    _r_info "lock-on-sleep's start timeout is not a plain number of seconds,"\
" so the lock edge's worst case is unchecked"
    return 0
  fi
  # +2 for the SIGKILL grace: a hook ignoring SIGTERM costs the full bound plus
  # the backstop before the traversal moves on.
  _per=$((HOOK_TIMEOUT + 2))
  _nblock=$(_rep_hookcount .block)
  _nact=$(_rep_hookcount act)
  _nall=$(_rep_hookcount .block act .report .verify)
  # Only what runs BEFORE the screen is locked counts against the lock.
  _ppos=$(_lock_provider_pos)
  if [ -n "$_ppos" ]; then _ncrit=$((_nblock + _ppos))
  else _ncrit=$((_nblock + _nact)); fi
  _wcrit=$((_ncrit * _per))
  _wall=$((_nall * _per))

  if [ "$_nall" -eq 0 ]; then
    _r_info "no hooks on the lock edge, so nothing can spend its budget"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$_wcrit" -gt "$_bsec" ]; then
    if [ -n "$_ppos" ]; then
      _r_warn "the LOCK itself can take ${_wcrit}s ($_ncrit hooks run before"\
" the provider finishes, x ${_per}s) against lock-on-sleep's ${_bsec}s. If"\
" they hang, systemd kills the unit mid-lock and logind suspends anyway."\
" Order the provider EARLIER in lock.d, lower VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT, or drop"\
" a hook ahead of it"
    else
      _r_warn "the LOCK itself can take ${_wcrit}s ($_ncrit block+act hooks x"\
" ${_per}s) against lock-on-sleep's ${_bsec}s. No hook in lock.d resolves into"\
" a providers/ directory, so every act hook is counted as running before the"\
" lock -- the safe assumption, not a measurement. Lower"\
" VIGILANCE_HOOK_TIMEOUT, drop a hook, or place the provider so it is"\
" identifiable"
    fi
  elif [ "$_wall" -gt "$_bsec" ]; then
    # INFO, not WARN, and the difference is deliberate. The LOCK fits, which is
    # the guarantee; what can be cut short is the verification after it. On a
    # normally-wired box the total exceeds the budget permanently (eight hooks
    # at the default bound is 96s against 25s) and no realistic wiring clears
    # it, so a warning here would be a line that is always on -- which is how a
    # report teaches you to skip the line that matters.
    _r_info "the lock edge can take ${_wall}s total ($_nall hooks x ${_per}s)"\
" against lock-on-sleep's ${_bsec}s. The lock itself fits (${_wcrit}s), so"\
" the screen locks; only a pathological run reaches this, and it would cost"\
" the verification rather than the lock"
  else
    _r_ok "lock edge worst case ${_wall}s fits lock-on-sleep's ${_bsec}s"\
" (${_ncrit} of $_nall hooks before the lock is done)"
    if [ -z "$_ppos" ] && [ "$_nact" -gt 1 ]; then
      _r_info "no hook in lock.d resolves into a providers/ directory, so the"\
" figure above assumes every act hook precedes the lock"
    fi
  fi
}

_rep_actuators() {
  echo "-- actuators --"
  _act_seen=0 _act_bad=0
  for _n in "$SYS_BACKLIGHT"/*/brightness \
            "$SYS_LEDS"/*kbd_backlight*/brightness \
            "$SYS_LEDS"/platform::mute/brightness \
            "$SYS_LEDS"/platform::micmute/brightness; do
    [ -e "$_n" ] || continue
    _act_seen=$((_act_seen + 1))
    if [ ! -w "$_n" ]; then
      _r_bad "NOT writable: $_n (hooks will silently no-op)"
      _act_bad=$((_act_bad + 1))
    fi
  done
  if [ "$_act_seen" = 0 ]; then
    # NAMES THE NODES, NOT A TOOL. The scan is over sysfs paths that are
    # themselves knobs, and hooklib is no longer brightnessctl-shaped
    # after 684a4ab, so blaming a tool for an absent DEVICE was both wrong
    # and the last mechanism name in a section that has none.
    _r_info "no writable backlight or LED nodes on this host (nothing"\
" to drive)"
  elif [ "$_act_bad" = 0 ]; then
    _r_ok "$_act_seen actuator node(s) writable"
  else
    # The remedy, named, because "denied" without it sends you reading source.
    _r_info "grant via udev/99-vigilance.rules + the 'vigilant' group; do NOT"\
" use 'input', which also grants raw keystroke read"
  fi
}

# Every executable block hook wired on an edge the runner will never consult.
# Both scopes, because a machine-scope one is just as inert and less visible.
_ascent_blocks() {
  for _ar in "$HOOK_ROOT" "$MACHINE_HOOK_ROOT"; do
    if [ -z "$_ar" ] || [ ! -d "$_ar" ]; then continue; fi
    for _ae in unlock wake resume; do
      if [ ! -d "$_ar/$_ae.block.d" ]; then continue; fi
      for _ah in "$_ar/$_ae.block.d"/*; do
        if [ -x "$_ah" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$_ah"; fi
      done
    done
  done
  return 0
}

_rep_wiring() {
  echo "-- wiring --"
  # BLOCKED HOOKS FIRST, because they are the finding and the listing below is
  # only context. A hook wired and unable to run is invisible to every other
  # tier: no edge is attempted, so nothing reaches the log, `verify` has nothing
  # to disagree with, and `audit` sees no event. The wiring is the ONLY place
  # that truth exists.
  _blk=$(_blocked_all)
  if [ -z "$_blk" ]; then
    _r_ok "every wired hook is runnable from this session"
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$_blk" | while IFS= read -r _b; do
      if [ -z "$_b" ]; then continue; fi
      printf '  [FAIL] wired but NOT runnable here: %s\n' "$_b"
    done
    # That loop is a subshell, so it cannot raise RRC from inside.
    RRC=1
    _r_info "a hook present but not executable does not FAIL, it does not"\
" EXIST for this session; check the target is reachable by the uid crossing"\
" the edge (a MACHINE-scope hook under a home is unreadable to a greeter)"
  fi
  # AND A BLOCK HOOK ON AN ASCENT EDGE, which the runner now refuses to honour.
  # Silently ignoring it would leave the operator believing a veto is armed that
  # does nothing, so the ignoring is stated where wiring errors are read.
  # STATE OUTSIDE A RUNTIME DIR is a different machine's answer waiting to
  # happen: nothing else writes there, so every rung reads as `open`.
  if [ -n "${RUN_DIR_FALLBACK:-}" ]; then
    _r_warn "no runtime dir found (XDG_RUNTIME_DIR unset, no"\
" $_rtbase/$_uid): state is in $RUN_DIR, which the session does not share."\
" Every rung reads as 'open' here regardless of where the machine is"
  fi
  # A MISSING timeout(1) silently removes the hook bound, so say so. Without
  # this the guarantee would be present on every box that has coreutils and
  # absent, with no trace, on one that does not.
  if [ -z "$HOOK_TMO" ] && [ "$HOOK_TIMEOUT" != 0 ]; then
    _r_warn "no timeout(1) on PATH: hooks run UNBOUNDED. A hook that hangs"\
" blocks every later hook on its edge, including the lock provider"
  fi
  _ab=$(_ascent_blocks)
  if [ -n "$_ab" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$_ab" | while IFS= read -r _b; do
      if [ -z "$_b" ]; then continue; fi
      printf '  [WARN] block hook on an ASCENT edge, IGNORED: %s\n' "$_b"
    done
    _r_info "the block tier may refuse a descent but never an ascent:"\
  " honouring it would leave the machine dark with its only way out vetoed."\
  " Move it to the matching DESCENT edge, or delete it"
  fi
  cmd_hooks 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/  /'

  echo "-- recent edges --"
  tail -12 "$LOG_FILE" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/  /' || echo "  (no log yet)"
}

# The orchestrator: WHAT is reported and in what order, with no section logic of
# its own. The order is not cosmetic. Session and recorded state come first
# because every later section's meaning depends on them ("is this claim even
# ours to make?"), and the log tail comes last so the evidence sits next to the
# verdict rather than scrolled off above it.
cmd_report() {
  RRC=0
  echo "== vigilance: $(hostname -s 2>/dev/null) $(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"
  _rep_session
  _rep_state
  _rep_hardware
  _rep_coherence
  _rep_verify
  _rep_coverage
  _rep_deadlines
  _rep_machinery
  _rep_actuators
  _rep_budget
  _rep_wiring
  return "$RRC"
}

# --- rescue: one key, when the screen is gone and you cannot see to type -----
# Bound to a bare unmodified key by the integrator, so it must be pressable
# BLIND. Two jobs in this order, and the order is the point: RECORD first, then
# recover. The fault is intermittent and never happens while instrumented, so
# the button IS the instrument; recovering first would burn the only evidence
# we get.
#
# It records by calling cmd_report, not by gathering its own facts. Those two
# drifting apart is exactly the failure this project is about, and the rescue
# log is worthless if it does not hold what `report` would have shown.
#
# IT ACTUATES NOTHING ITSELF. Recovery is `force open`, which crosses every
# ascent edge and runs the hooks; anything it should drive belongs in a hook,
# where it is one implementation instead of two. The direct wlopm/DDC/kbd
# fallbacks this replaced had drifted into duplicates of the hooks they shadow,
# and its swaylock signal had become a lock-killer on a stock binary.
cmd_rescue() {
  _rl=${VIGILANCE_RESCUE_LOG:-$(dirname "$LOG_FILE")/vigilance-rescue.log}
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$_rl")" 2>/dev/null || true
  {
    echo ""
    echo "=========================================================="
    echo "=== vigilant rescue $(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"
    echo "uptime: $(uptime -p 2>/dev/null)"
    echo "boot:   $(cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id 2>/dev/null)"
    cmd_report || true
    # Rescue-only, because it is too heavy for a routine report and only
    # matters when something is actually wrong.
    echo "-- compositor --"
    timeout 5 wlopm 2>&1 | sed 's/^/  /'
    timeout 5 wlr-randr 2>&1 | sed -n '1,20p;s/^/  /'
    echo "-- journal, last 3 min --"
    timeout 5 journalctl --since "-3 min" --no-pager 2>/dev/null \
      | grep -iE "drm|i915|xe |wayfire|swaylock|backlight|dpms|connector" \
      | tail -25 | sed 's/^/  /'
  } >> "$_rl" 2>&1

  _log "RESCUE pressed; evidence in $_rl"
  _rrc=0
  cmd_go open force || _rrc=$?
  {
    echo "-- after recovery (rc=$_rrc) --"
    cmd_report || true
    echo "=== end ==="
  } >> "$_rl" 2>&1

  # A human pressing the panic key IS an intervention, whether or not the
  # recovery worked. Raise it through the alert tier rather than calling any
  # one host's notifier: that is what the tier is for, and it is how this stops
  # hardcoding an integrator's tooling.
  _alert rescue "screen rescue pressed; evidence in $_rl"
  echo "vigilant: rescue done (rc=$_rrc); evidence in $_rl" >&2
  return "$_rrc"
}

# --- due: when SHOULD this edge have fired? ---------------------------------
# THE TIMEOUT LIVES OUTSIDE VIGILANT. swaylock.conf has `blank-after`, swayidle
# has its own timers, a greeter has another; vigilant must not hold a second
# copy of any of them, because two copies of a timeout is just a slower way to
# be wrong. So a mechanism-specific hook READS the real config and reports a
# deadline, and vigilant stays mechanism-agnostic.
#
# CONTRACT: a due.d hook prints
#
#     <seconds> [anchor]
#
# and exits 0. No output, junk, or a non-zero exit means "no opinion", which is
# not the same as "zero". MAX across hooks, because the latest claimed deadline
# is the one nobody can call premature.
#
# THE ANCHOR IS LOAD-BEARING and was missing from the first cut, which cost a
# false alarm on a healthy box. `rung` (the default) means the clock starts when
# the previous rung was entered. `idle` means it starts at the last INPUT, which
# is what every swayidle timer actually does.
#
# An idle-anchored deadline is NOT ENFORCEABLE here, because vigilant has no
# idle-time source: logind reports IdleHint=no and IdleSinceHint=0 on this
# stack, and wlroots exposes no CLI for it. Measured from rung entry instead,
# `lock` read as 1616s overdue on a machine being actively typed on -- the
# enforce timer would have alerted every minute, forever, about nothing.
#
# So an idle anchor is reported and deliberately NOT acted on. The forensic tier
# is the right home for those edges: `audit` asks whether the timer that DID
# fire produced its edge, which needs no idle clock at all.
#
# BUT THAT IS A DESIGN, NOT A DELIVERY, and this comment used to claim otherwise
# ("audit covers it"). No shipped audit source reads idle events: the only one
# reads logind SLEEP events, and swayidle logs nothing a hook could reconcile
# unless it is running with -d. So an idle-anchored edge with no audit source is
# watched by NOTHING, and saying "audit covers it" was the same comforting
# untruth as an `enabled` unit that cannot execute.
#
# _audit_covered exists so the runtime can say which of the two situations it is
# actually in, rather than making the reader trust a comment.
_audit_covered() {   # -> 0 if ANY audit source is wired
  [ -n "$(_hooks_for audit)" ]
}
# --- the idle clock --------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE MEASUREMENT THIS SUITE HAS NEVER HAD, and the reason a whole tier was
# inert. The deadline that matters -- "the screen should have locked by now" --
# is expressed in IDLE time, and vigilant could not read idle time. The only
# clock it had was time-since-entering-the-rung, which on an actively used
# machine over-reports: measured from rung entry it once called `lock` 1616s
# overdue while the machine was being typed on.
#
# So `idle` was refused outright, and refusing it made the SOON question
# unanswerable, which made `enforce` unreachable. Three layers of machinery
# resting on one missing number.
#
# IT IS A HOOK, not a built-in, for the reason everything here is a hook: the
# answer is compositor-specific. Wayfire advertises ext_idle_notifier_v1; a
# greeter running sway may answer differently; a headless box cannot answer at
# all. vigilance ships the extension POINT and the integrator supplies the
# mechanism.
#
# CONTRACT: print seconds-idle on stdout and exit 0. Exit 78 for "I cannot
# tell" -- which is NOT zero, because zero means "input one second ago" and is
# the single most dangerous wrong answer here: it would silently reset every
# deadline forever and report a healthy machine.
_idle_secs() {   # -> seconds since last seat input, or empty if unknowable
  _il=$(_hooks_for idle)
  if [ -z "$_il" ]; then return 0; fi
  _imin=
  _iifs=$IFS; IFS='
'
  for _ih in $_il; do
    IFS=$_iifs
    if [ -z "$_ih" ]; then continue; fi
    _io=$RUN_DIR/idle.$$
    _irc=0
    # Through a FILE, not $(...): a command substitution waits for the pipe to
    # close rather than for the hook to exit, so a source leaving a child on
    # stdout defeats the bound entirely. Measured at 21s against a 2s timeout.
    # A STATE DIR AND A PATH, like every other tier. An idle source is
    # inherently STATEFUL -- the only unprivileged way to read idle time is to
    # sample a monotonic counter and remember what it said last time -- so a
    # source with nowhere to remember is a source that can only ever decline.
    _isd=$STATE_ROOT/$(basename "$_ih")
    mkdir -p "$_isd" 2>/dev/null || true
    VIGILANCE_KIND=idle VIGILANCE_STATE_DIR="$_isd" PATH="$HOOK_PATH" \
      $HOOK_TMO "$_ih" > "$_io" 2>/dev/null || _irc=$?
    _iv=$(cat "$_io" 2>/dev/null | head -1 | tr -dc '0-9')
    rm -f "$_io"
    if [ "$_irc" != 0 ] || [ -z "$_iv" ]; then continue; fi
    # THE MINIMUM, deliberately. Several sources should agree; when they do not,
    # the one reporting the LEAST idle time is the one that saw input most
    # recently, and believing it is what prevents a stale source manufacturing
    # a false "overdue" against a machine somebody is sitting at.
    if [ -z "$_imin" ] || [ "$_iv" -lt "$_imin" ]; then _imin=$_iv; fi
    IFS='
'
  done
  IFS=$_iifs
  # THE LADDER'S OWN RECORD IS A CEILING ON IDLE TIME, and a ceiling is NOT a
  # source: it bounds an answer, it cannot supply one. So it is applied after
  # the minimum and only when something actually answered -- a box with no idle
  # source still reports nothing, which is what keeps `report` honest about the
  # deadline being unmeasurable.
  #
  # An ASCENT means something raised the machine, and whatever did also restarts
  # the clock of the mechanism being judged: swayidle rearms every timeout on a
  # resume. Our counters can miss the cause entirely -- a lid switch is not seat
  # input, and `systemctl restart vigilance-idle.service` makes swayidle run its
  # pending resume commands on SIGTERM, which is documented behaviour and not a
  # bug. Both raise the ladder while /proc/interrupts sits perfectly still.
  #
  # Measured on manifold: a tackup run restarted the idle unit at 23:46:51, the
  # shutdown resume crossed `wake`, and the overdue detector then alerted once a
  # minute that `sleep` was 3000s late -- about a freshly armed timer that was
  # going to fire on schedule. Without the ceiling the finding is not "the
  # mechanism is broken" but "our clock is older than the mechanism's".
  if [ -n "$_imin" ]; then
    _ia=$(_last_ascent)
    if [ "$_ia" -gt 0 ]; then
      _isince=$(( $(date +%s) - _ia ))
      if [ "$_isince" -lt 0 ]; then _isince=0; fi
      if [ "$_isince" -lt "$_imin" ]; then _imin=$_isince; fi
    fi
  fi
  printf '%s' "$_imin"
}

_due_for() {   # edge from -> "seconds [anchor]", or empty for no opinion
  _dl=$(_hooks_for "$1" .due)
  if [ -z "$_dl" ]; then return 0; fi
  _dmax= _danchor=rung
  while IFS= read -r _dh; do
    if [ -z "$_dh" ]; then continue; fi
    _drc=0
    # Bounded: `enforce` runs this every minute forever, and a due hook that
    # hangs wedges the supervision loop that exists to notice things not
    # happening. These read a config file; they have no reason to be slow.
    # Captured through a file rather than `$(...)` -- see _blocked for why a
    # substitution silently defeats the bound.
    mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true
    _dcf=$RUN_DIR/.due.$$
    VIGILANCE_EDGE="$1" VIGILANCE_FROM="$2" \
      VIGILANCE_KIND=due $HOOK_TMO "$_dh" "$1" "$2" >"$_dcf" 2>/dev/null \
      || _drc=$?
    _draw=$(cat "$_dcf" 2>/dev/null || true)
    rm -f "$_dcf" 2>/dev/null || true
    # "No opinion" and "could not answer" are the same VERDICT here by contract
    # (a missing deadline must not alarm), but they are not the same EVENT, and
    # the record is where that distinction has to survive. A due hook that
    # stopped being able to read its config degrades silently to "nothing to
    # enforce" -- green, because nothing is overdue when no deadline exists.
    # Logging it is what makes that traceable later without changing the
    # verdict and spamming a timer.
    if [ "$_drc" -ne 0 ]; then
      _log "due hook $(basename "$_dh") errored (rc=$_drc); treated as no"\
" opinion, so no deadline is claimed for $1"
      continue
    fi
    _dv=$(printf '%s' "$_draw" | awk '{print $1}')
    _da=$(printf '%s' "$_draw" | awk '{print $2}')
    case "$_dv" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
    if [ -z "$_dmax" ] || [ "$_dv" -gt "$_dmax" ]; then _dmax=$_dv; fi
    # ANY idle-anchored hook makes the whole deadline unenforceable: we cannot
    # tell an idle machine from a busy one, so acting would be a guess.
    case "$_da" in idle) _danchor=idle ;; esac
  done <<EOF
$_dl
EOF
  [ -n "$_dmax" ] || return 0
  printf '%s %s' "$_dmax" "$_danchor"
}

# The next DESCENT edge from a rung, or empty at the bottom.
#
# `suspend` is deliberately excluded as an enforcement target. Forcing that edge
# would fire suspend.d without the machine actually suspending, leaving the
# peripherals configured for S3 on a box that is awake. And vigilant starting
# a real power transition would mean competing with the trust root instead of
# riding it, which is the one thing this design refuses to do.
_next_down() {   # rung -> the edge below it, or empty
  # The descent edge of the NEXT rung down, minus anything policy refuses to
  # drive to. Structure and policy stay apart: `suspend` is a real edge that
  # simply is not ours to force.
  _nd=$(_lt | awk -v r="$1" '$1 == r { hit = 1; next } hit { print $2; exit }')
  if [ -z "${_nd:-}" ] || [ "$_nd" = - ]; then return 0; fi
  for _ne in $NEVER_ENFORCED; do
    if [ "$_nd" = "$_ne" ]; then return 0; fi
  done
  printf '%s\n' "$_nd"
}

cmd_due() {   # [edge]
  _from=$(_depth)
  _e=${1:-$(_next_down "$_from")}
  if [ -z "$_e" ]; then
    echo "due: nothing below '$_from' is an enforcement target"
    return 0
  fi
  _draw=$(_due_for "$_e" "$_from")
  if [ -z "$_draw" ]; then
    echo "due $_e: n/a (no due hook has an opinion)"
    return 0
  fi
  _d=$(printf '%s' "$_draw" | awk '{print $1}')
  _anch=$(printf '%s' "$_draw" | awk '{print $2}')
  # AN IDLE ANCHOR IS ANSWERABLE when something can answer it. `due` only ever
  # reports, so this adds the measured number and nothing else; the finding
  # itself belongs to enforce, which is what runs on a timer.
  if [ "$_anch" = idle ]; then
    _idle=$(_idle_secs)
    if [ -n "$_idle" ]; then
      if [ "$_idle" -le $((_d + GRACE)) ]; then
        echo "due $_e: ${_d}s after last input; idle ${_idle}s, not due yet"
      else
        echo "due $_e: ${_d}s after last input; idle ${_idle}s, OVERDUE"
      fi
      return 0
    fi
  fi
  if [ "$_anch" = idle ]; then
    # NAME WHAT IS ACTUALLY WATCHING IT. "see audit for these edges" read as
    # reassurance while no audit source for them existed.
    if _audit_covered; then
      echo "due $_e: ${_d}s after last INPUT, which vigilant cannot observe;"\
" reported but NOT enforceable (the audit tier reconciles it after the fact)"
    else
      echo "due $_e: ${_d}s after last INPUT, which vigilant cannot observe;"\
" NOT enforceable AND no audit source is wired, so nothing is watching this"\
" edge at all"
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  _at=$(_depth_at)
  if [ "$_at" -gt 0 ]; then
    _el=$(( $(date +%s) - _at ))
    echo "due $_e: ${_d}s after '$_from' (entered ${_el}s ago, grace ${GRACE}s)"
  else
    echo "due $_e: ${_d}s after '$_from' (no depth record; not overdue)"
  fi
}

# --- enforce: the supervision loop, meant for a timer -----------------------
# This is the second pillar. Pillar one is "make the things go dark"; this is
# "notice when the mechanism that does it has silently stopped working". A
# primary mechanism that quietly dies currently costs weeks of dead peripherals
# and nobody finds out; the goal is to turn that into a RECORDED event.
#
# GRACE exists so the primary mechanism wins the race in the normal case.
# vigilant acts only when an edge is demonstrably overdue, never on the tie.
#
# POLICY, and this is a deliberate narrowing of the plan. `report` (the default)
# logs and alerts; `force` also crosses the edge. The plan's end state is
# force, and its own safety constraint is "never force a descent without a
# reliable ascent" -- vigilant cannot see input while locked, so a forced blank
# could land mid-password. The rescue key is that floor and now exists, but it
# has been pressed in anger exactly never, so forcing stays opt-in until an
# ascent has been proven on the box in question. An alert that names the failure
# already buys the charter's goal; forcing buys convenience on top of it.
# THE STANDING RE-CHECK. Verification used to happen only when an edge was
# crossed, so a machine that reached a rung correctly and then DRIFTED out of it
# went unnoticed until the next crossing -- which, at `sleep`, might be hours.
# Worse, a mechanism that never worked at all was only ever asked once, at the
# moment it was least likely to have failed yet.
#
# This runs on the supervision timer, so the question "is the machine still
# where it says it is" gets asked every minute instead of once. It is the
# second, independent path to the same truth: the edge-time verify says a
# crossing went well, this says the state is STILL true.
#
# Safe to run this often only because alerts now dedup on onset -- without that
# a persistent fault would notify every minute, and the enforce timer would be
# switched off by hand exactly as it was once before.
#
# It REPORTS, it does not act. Acting on a drifted rung is cmd_go's business and
# is gated by the enforcement policy below; this only refuses to let the drift
# be silent.
# --- watchdog: is the SUPERVISING MACHINERY itself alive? -------------------
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT AN audit.d HOOK, which is the obvious place to reach for. The
# audit contract is `<epoch> <edge> <note>` and means "this event happened; did
# its edge follow?". It is EVENT-DRIVEN. A watchdog is the inverse: nothing
# happened when something should have, so there is no event to report and no
# timestamp to reconcile. Squeezing it in would mean emitting a fake event, and
# a forensic tier fed invented events is worse than no watchdog at all.
#
# HOW IT CAN WORK AT ALL. A wedged daemon is RUNNING: the process is alive, the
# argv is right, the unit is active. Every liveness check in this suite passes.
# Catching that needs two things, and neither is an idle clock:
#
#   1. A HEARTBEAT IT CANNOT AVOID EMITTING WHEN HEALTHY. swayidle-mgr arms a
#      short observational timeout, so a healthy timer emits on every brief
#      pause -- turning a rare event (a full idle-lock, which can legitimately
#      not happen all day) into a frequent one. Only then is silence evidence.
#
#   2. AN OBSERVER THAT CANNOT SHARE ITS FATE. That is the part people get
#      wrong. The heartbeat SHOULD share fate with the thing under test -- that
#      is exactly why it stops when the thing stops. What must be independent
#      is whoever LOOKS at it, and we already have that: a systemd timer, which
#      is the trust root this project already leans on everywhere else.
#
# So the shape is the classic one: the subject emits, a separate timer reads,
# and silence past a threshold is the finding. A hook exits 0 for healthy, 1
# for "the thing I watch is not doing its job", 78 for "I cannot tell".
#
# Alert dedup on onset is what makes running it every minute safe; without it a
# persistent fault notifies sixty times an hour, and the timer gets stopped by
# hand, which has already happened once on a live box.
_run_watchdogs() {   # -> 0 healthy or unknown, 1 something is not doing its job
  _wl=$(_hooks_for watchdog)
  if [ -z "$_wl" ]; then return 0; fi
  _wrc=0
  _wifs=$IFS; IFS='
'
  for _wh in $_wl; do
    IFS=$_wifs
    if [ -z "$_wh" ]; then continue; fi
    _wo=$RUN_DIR/.wd.$$
    _whrc=0
    _wsd=$STATE_ROOT/$(basename "$_wh")
    mkdir -p "$_wsd" 2>/dev/null || true
    VIGILANCE_KIND=watchdog VIGILANCE_STATE_DIR="$_wsd" \
      PATH="$HOOK_PATH" $HOOK_TMO "$_wh" >"$_wo" 2>/dev/null || _whrc=$?
    _wmsg=$(head -1 "$_wo" 2>/dev/null || true)
    rm -f "$_wo" 2>/dev/null || true
    case "$_whrc" in
      0|"$HOOK_NA") ;;
      *)
        _wrc=1
        _log "WATCHDOG $(basename "$_wh"): ${_wmsg:-not doing its job}"
        echo "watchdog $(basename "$_wh"): ${_wmsg:-not doing its job}"
        _alert watchdog "${_wmsg:-$(basename "$_wh") reports its subject is"\
" not doing its job}" ;;
    esac
    IFS='
'
  done
  IFS=$_wifs
  return "$_wrc"
}

# ONE RECORD IS ONE LINE, and a captured verify transcript is not one line.
#
# `log()` writes to stderr AND to the log file, so `cmd_verify 2>&1` captures
# vigilant's own already-logged messages alongside the hook's. Feeding that
# straight into _log wrote a record whose FIRST line carried a timestamp and
# whose remaining lines carried none -- observed live:
#
#   2026-09-20T21:21:50 STILL-DRIFTED at 'sleep': expected dark, found 1/2
#   vigilant: HOOK FAILED (rc=1): sleep.verify 30-kbd-backlight
#   verify sleep: FAIL
#
# The audit tier parses this file by line, so untimestamped debris is exactly
# the kind of thing that makes a forensic reader mis-read a window. The
# `vigilant: ` lines are dropped rather than joined because `log()` already
# wrote each of them as its own proper record: repeating them here is pure
# duplication, not context.
_flatten() {   # multi-line transcript -> one line, vigilant's own echo removed
  printf '%s' "$1" | grep -v "^$PKG: " | tr '\n' ' ' \
    | sed 's/  */ /g; s/^ //; s/ $//'
}

_enforce_recheck() {   # from
  # A CROSSING IN FLIGHT IS NOT DRIFT.
  #
  # The verify tier takes a second or more -- a ddcutil round trip, a screen
  # capture -- and this runs on a one-minute timer, so a crossing can start and
  # finish inside a single recheck. The verdict then describes a rung the
  # machine has already LEFT, with the hardware correctly matching the new one.
  #
  # Observed on manifold, and the log shows it plainly:
  #
  #   22:24:01  cross wake: sleep -> lock       (hooks restore the LEDs to lit)
  #   22:24:02  HOOK FAILED: sleep.verify 30-kbd-backlight
  #   22:24:02  HOOK FAILED: sleep.verify 40-mute-leds
  #   22:24:03  STILL-DRIFTED at 'sleep'
  #
  # The minute BEFORE was clean, which is what rules out genuinely-lit LEDs at
  # the sleep rung: the same question passed at 22:23 and failed one second
  # after the wake. A crossing takes a few seconds and the timer fires every
  # sixty, so this misfires on a few percent of wakes -- often enough to train
  # a reader that drift alerts are noise, which is the one thing this tier
  # cannot afford.
  #
  # So the depth AND the moment it was recorded are captured before and after.
  # If either moved, an edge was crossed while we were looking and the answer
  # is about the wrong rung. Discard it: the next pass will ask again, and a
  # real drift persists.
  # AND A CROSSING THAT IS STILL RUNNING IS NOT DRIFT EITHER, which the
  # depth-moved check above cannot see: the depth is committed BEFORE the act
  # tier, so mid-crossing it is both correct and perfectly still. Asked before
  # the verify, not after, because the hook-failure alert is raised from INSIDE
  # cmd_verify -- discarding the verdict afterwards would still have toasted.
  if _crossing_inflight; then
    echo "enforce: a crossing is in flight; no verdict"
    return 0
  fi
  _rq_d=$(_depth); _rq_t=$(_depth_at)
  _rv=$(cmd_verify 2>&1) || _rvrc=$?
  _rv=$(_flatten "$_rv")
  if [ "$(_depth)" != "$_rq_d" ] || [ "$(_depth_at)" != "$_rq_t" ] \
     || _crossing_inflight; then
    echo "enforce: an edge was crossed while verifying '$_rq_d'; no verdict"
    return 0
  fi
  case "${_rvrc:-0}" in
    0) return 0 ;;
    # NOT DRIFT. Drift means we looked and the machine was wrong; 78 means
    # nothing is wired to look. Raising it here would alert every minute about
    # a static wiring fact -- and an alert that fires on a correct machine is
    # how the enforce timer got stopped by hand once already. `report` owns
    # this finding and states it ONCE, so it is neither lost nor a storm.
    "$HOOK_NA") return 0 ;;
  esac
  echo "enforce: $_rv"
  _log "STILL-DRIFTED at '$1': $_rv"
  _alert drift "the machine is at '$1' but no longer matches it: $_rv"
  return 1
}

cmd_enforce() {
  mkdir -p "$RUN_DIR" "$STATE_ROOT"
  _from=$(_depth)
  _at=$(_depth_at)
  if [ "$_at" -le 0 ]; then
    echo "enforce: no depth record; nothing is overdue"
    return 0
  fi
  # Asked FIRST, and unconditionally: whether a deadline exists is a separate
  # question from whether the machine is still in the state it claims, and
  # every early return below would otherwise skip it.
  _erc=0
  _enforce_recheck "$_from" || _erc=1
  # UNCONDITIONAL, for the same reason the standing recheck is: whether a
  # deadline exists is a different question from whether the machinery that
  # would meet it is alive, and every early return below would skip it.
  _run_watchdogs || _erc=1
  _e=$(_next_down "$_from")
  if [ -z "$_e" ]; then
    echo "enforce: nothing below '$_from' is an enforcement target"
    return "$_erc"
  fi
  _draw=$(_due_for "$_e" "$_from")
  if [ -z "$_draw" ]; then
    echo "enforce: $_e has no declared deadline; nothing to enforce"
    return "$_erc"
  fi
  _d=$(printf '%s' "$_draw" | awk '{print $1}')
  _anch=$(printf '%s' "$_draw" | awk '{print $2}')
  # An idle-anchored deadline starts at the last INPUT. vigilant could not read
  # that, so "overdue" meant only "this rung has been held a while" -- which on
  # an actively used machine is normal: measured from rung entry it called
  # `lock` 1616s overdue while the machine was being typed on, and the timer
  # would have alerted every minute, forever, about nothing.
  #
  # AN idle.d SOURCE MAKES IT MEASURABLE. The refusal further down is kept for
  # when nothing can answer, because a guess there is worse than an abstention.
  if [ "$_anch" = idle ]; then
    _idle=$(_idle_secs)
    if [ -n "$_idle" ]; then
      if [ "$_idle" -le $((_d + GRACE)) ]; then
        echo "enforce: $_e not due yet (idle ${_idle}s of $((_d + GRACE))s)"
        return "$_erc"
      fi
      if _blocked "$_e" "$_from"; then
        _log "OVERDUE $_e: idle ${_idle}s of ${_d}s, but blocked; holding off"
        echo "enforce: $_e overdue but blocked; holding off"
        return "$_erc"
      fi
      # THE FINDING THIS TIER EXISTS FOR, finally expressible: the seat has
      # been quiet past the deadline and the edge did not happen. A WEDGED
      # idle timer is running, correctly armed and silent -- it passes the
      # argv check, logs no event for audit to reconcile, and leaves the
      # machine at a rung it genuinely matches. Nothing else here can see it,
      # and it is the failure that created this package.
      _log "OVERDUE $_e: seat idle ${_idle}s against a ${_d}s deadline and"\
" the edge has not fired"
      _alert overdue "$_e should have fired ${_idle}s into a ${_d}s idle"\
" deadline; the primary mechanism for it is not working"
      echo "enforce: $_e OVERDUE (idle ${_idle}s of ${_d}s); reported"
      return 1
    fi
  fi
  if [ "$_anch" = idle ]; then
    if _audit_covered; then
      echo "enforce: $_e is idle-anchored; not enforceable here, left to audit"
      return "$_erc"
    fi
    # UNWATCHED, and that is worth saying once rather than silently returning 0
    # every minute. Not an alert: it is a wiring gap, not an event, and alerting
    # on it every minute is how a human learns to ignore the channel.
    echo "enforce: $_e is idle-anchored and NOT enforceable, and no audit"\
" source is wired for it; this edge is unsupervised"
    return 0
  fi
  _el=$(( $(date +%s) - _at ))
  if [ "$_el" -le $((_d + GRACE)) ]; then
    echo "enforce: $_e not due yet (${_el}s of $((_d + GRACE))s)"
    return "$_erc"
  fi

  # OVERDUE. Ask whether something is legitimately preventing it before
  # concluding the mechanism failed: an idle inhibitor or a fullscreen video is
  # the mechanism behaving correctly, and fighting that would be the bug.
  if _blocked "$_e" "$_from"; then
    _log "OVERDUE $_e by $((_el - _d))s, but blocked; holding off"
    echo "enforce: $_e overdue but blocked; holding off"
    return "$_erc"
  fi

  _alert overdue "$_e is ${_el}s into a ${_d}s deadline and has not fired;"\
" the primary mechanism for it is not working"
  _enforce_act "$_e" "$_el" "$_d"
}

# THE ACTING HALF, reached only when an edge is demonstrably overdue, nothing is
# blocking it, and the human has already been alerted. Split from the guard
# chain above because the two halves answer different questions: everything
# before this decides "is anything wrong?", and every one of its exits is a
# HEALTHY state.
# This decides "what, if anything, do we do about it?" -- and is the only part
# that can change the machine.
# THE REPORTING HALF. It used to have an ACTING half too, behind
# VIGILANCE_ENFORCE=force, and that is RETIRED (2026-09-20).
#
# WHY IT WENT. It was unreachable by construction, and it had been superseded
# without anyone noticing. Unreachable: only two edges are enforcement targets,
# `sleep` is refused outright because a forced descent into a dark rung arms no
# way back, and `lock` was refused because its deadline is idle-anchored -- so
# the intersection of "forceable" and "measurable" was empty, and the force
# path was tested code that could never run in production.
#
# SUPERSEDED: it was designed before five mechanisms grew up around it, and
# every failure it would have acted on is now caught closer to the cause. A
# misarmed idle timer is caught by report reading /proc/<pid>/cmdline; a failing
# provider by hook-failure alerting; a lock that did not take by `verify` and
# locker-up; continuous drift by the standing recheck; the past by audit.
#
# What was left uncovered was one sliver -- the idle timer alive, correctly
# armed, and silently not firing -- and that wants DETECTING, not forcing.
# Detection is now possible because the idle clock exists (see _idle_secs), and
# forcing would have added an actuator on the security path driven by a
# measurement, for nothing the alert does not already give.
_enforce_act() {   # edge elapsed deadline
  _e=$1; _el=$2; _d=$3
  _log "OVERDUE $_e by $((_el - _d))s; reported"
  echo "enforce: $_e OVERDUE by $((_el - _d))s; reported"
  return 1
}

# --- audit: did every external event actually produce its edge? --------------
# THE THIRD QUESTION, and the one that found the bug this tier was built for.
#
#   verify   does the hardware match what we believe, NOW?
#   due      is an edge pending and overdue?
#   audit    did an event that ALREADY HAPPENED produce the edge it should have?
#
# Neither of the first two can see the failure that prompted this: logind slept
# the machine, lock-on-sleep.service was asked to lock it first, the unit
# 203/EXEC'd on a bad ExecStart, and systemd reached sleep.target and slept
# anyway. Nothing was in a wrong state afterwards, and nothing was pending --
# an EVENT simply produced no EDGE, and only history shows that.
#
# It went unnoticed for a whole refactor because the unit was `enabled` and
# `canonical`, both of which we checked, and neither of which says the binary it
# names exists. Forensics is the only tier that would have caught it.
#
# CONTRACT: an audit.d hook reads ONE event source and prints zero or more
#
#     <epoch> <edge> [note]
#
# meaning "at <epoch> something happened that should have produced <edge>".
# vigilant owns the reconciliation, so a hook never has to parse the edge log --
# the same split as `due`, where the hook reports a deadline and vigilant does
# the arithmetic. Mechanism knowledge (journalctl, logind, a timer) stays in the
# plugin; vigilance's own record stays vigilance's to read.
AUDIT_WINDOW=${VIGILANCE_AUDIT_WINDOW:-120}

# Did the log record <edge> within the window around <epoch>?
#
# ISO-8601 sorts lexicographically, so the window becomes a string comparison
# and this needs neither `date` per log line nor awk's mktime (absent from
# mawk).
#
# THREE WAYS TO SATISFY ONE ASSERTION, because the assertion is really about the
# RUNG: a descent edge shares its name with the rung it enters, and there is
# more than one correct route to being there.
#
#   cross lock:            it crossed that edge
#   already at 'lock'      it was there; the edge's purpose was already achieved
#   cross wake: sleep -> lock
#                          it ARRIVED at the rung by another edge
#
# The third was missing and the live journal found it. On resume, `go lock` is a
# no-op only when the depth file survived at `lock`; when the box had descended
# to `sleep` first -- the ORDINARY idle path -- it crosses `wake` to get back
# up. Both outcomes mean the session is locked, and only one of them was
# being counted, so every resume from the normal idle path was reported as a
# MISS. Same cries-wolf failure as the `due` anchor, one tier over.
_log_has_edge() {   # epoch edge
  [ -r "$LOG_FILE" ] || return 1
  _lo=$(date -d "@$(( $1 - AUDIT_WINDOW ))" '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S' 2>/dev/null) \
    || return 1
  _hi=$(date -d "@$(( $1 + AUDIT_WINDOW ))" '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S' 2>/dev/null) \
    || return 1
  awk -v lo="$_lo" -v hi="$_hi" -v e="$2" '
    $1 >= lo && $1 <= hi {
      if ($0 ~ ("cross " e ":")) { found = 1 }
      if ($0 ~ ("already at ." e ".")) { found = 1 }
      # ARRIVED at the rung by another edge. The trailing guard stops `lock`
      # matching a longer rung name, and tolerates the " (forced)" suffix.
      if ($0 ~ ("-> " e "( |$)")) { found = 1 }
      # ALREADY DEEPER, and so already locked: a Session.Lock arriving at
      # `sleep` is declined rather than answered by raising the machine, and
      # that decline satisfies the assertion just as firmly. Anchored on the
      # ASSERTED rung, which is why cmd_go names both in that line.
      if ($0 ~ ("deeper than ." e ".")) { found = 1 }
    }
    END { exit(found ? 0 : 1) }' "$LOG_FILE"
}

cmd_audit() {
  _al=$(_hooks_for audit)
  if [ -z "$_al" ]; then
    echo "audit: n/a (no audit hooks installed; no event source is reconciled)"
    return 0
  fi
  _checked=0 _missed=0 _broken=0
  while IFS= read -r _ah; do
    if [ -z "$_ah" ]; then continue; fi
    _sd=$STATE_ROOT/$(basename "$_ah")
    mkdir -p "$_sd" 2>/dev/null || true
    _arc=0
    # Bounded, but on ITS OWN larger budget. An audit source reads an external
    # event log -- the shipped one runs journalctl over a day of records -- and
    # a big journal legitimately takes longer than anything on the lock path is
    # allowed to. Sharing the edge bound would turn a healthy slow read into
    # "audit BROKEN" and retire the forensic tier by crying wolf. Nothing waits
    # on this: it is a daily timer, so latency is not the property to protect.
    # Captured through a file rather than `$(...)`, for the reason spelled out
    # in _blocked: a substitution waits on the pipe, so a source that leaves a
    # child holding stdout would hold the tier open past its bound.
    _acf=$RUN_DIR/.audit.$$
    VIGILANCE_KIND=audit VIGILANCE_STATE_DIR="$_sd" \
      PATH="$HOOK_PATH" $AUDIT_TMO "$_ah" >"$_acf" 2>/dev/null || _arc=$?
    _out=$(cat "$_acf" 2>/dev/null || true)
    rm -f "$_acf" 2>/dev/null || true
    # A BROKEN SOURCE IS NOT "NO EVENTS". This was a bare `|| continue`, so a
    # hook that could not read its source (no journal access, a renamed unit, a
    # parse error) left audit printing "nothing to reconcile" and exiting 0: a
    # green forensic tier that had checked nothing. Precisely the false green
    # the EMPTY VERIFY TIER was fixed for, reproduced one tier over -- and worse
    # here, because audit is the tier of last resort. If it is blind, nothing
    # else is looking.
    # A SOURCE THAT CANNOT LOOK IS NOT A BROKEN ONE. `journalctl` absent on a
    # non-systemd box, or a source whose event log was never created, has
    # nothing to reconcile and nothing wrong with it. Reporting that as BROKEN
    # is the crying-wolf half of the same conflation 78 exists to break -- and
    # this tier is the last resort, so teaching a reader to discount it is
    # expensive.
    if [ "$_arc" = "$HOOK_NA" ]; then
      echo "audit n/a: $(basename "$_ah") has no event source here"\
" (nothing to reconcile, nothing wrong)"
      continue
    fi
    if [ "$_arc" -ne 0 ]; then
      _broken=$((_broken + 1))
      _log "AUDIT: source $(basename "$_ah") failed (rc=$_arc); not reconciled"
      echo "audit BROKEN: $(basename "$_ah") could not read its event source"\
" (rc=$_arc); nothing from it was reconciled"
      continue
    fi
    while IFS= read -r _ev; do
      if [ -z "$_ev" ]; then continue; fi
      _when=$(printf '%s' "$_ev" | awk '{print $1}')
      _edge=$(printf '%s' "$_ev" | awk '{print $2}')
      _note=$(printf '%s' "$_ev" | cut -d' ' -f3-)
      case "$_when" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
      [ -n "$_edge" ] || continue
      _checked=$((_checked + 1))
      if _log_has_edge "$_when" "$_edge"; then
        echo "audit ok: $_edge at $(date -d "@$_when" '+%F %T') ${_note:-}"
      else
        _missed=$((_missed + 1))
        echo "audit MISS: $_edge expected at"\
" $(date -d "@$_when" '+%F %T') but no edge was crossed ${_note:-}"
      fi
    done <<EOF2
$_out
EOF2
  done <<EOF
$_al
EOF
  _audit_verdict
}

# The verdict, split out so cmd_audit stays readable. Three outcomes, and they
# are deliberately NOT collapsed: "no events" is healthy, "a source is blind" is
# a fault in the auditor itself, and "an event produced no edge" is a fault in
# the thing being audited. Reporting the second as the first is what made this
# tier lie; reporting it as the third would send a human hunting the wrong bug.
_audit_verdict() {
  if [ "$_broken" -gt 0 ]; then
    # The AUDITOR is broken, which is worth more alarm than a miss: a miss is
    # one known failure, a blind source is an unknown number of them.
    _alert audit-broken "$_broken audit source(s) could not be read; the"\
" forensic tier is partly blind and cannot be trusted green"
    _log "AUDIT: $_broken source(s) unreadable"
  fi
  if [ "$_missed" -gt 0 ]; then
    # An event that produced no edge means a TRIGGER or HANDLER did not fire.
    # That is exactly what the human needs told, and it is historical, so the
    # alert is the only thing that will reach them.
    _alert audit-miss "$_missed of $_checked external event(s) produced no"\
" edge; a trigger or handler is not firing"
    _log "AUDIT: $_missed of $_checked events produced no edge"
  fi
  if [ "$_missed" -gt 0 ] || [ "$_broken" -gt 0 ]; then return 1; fi
  if [ "$_checked" = 0 ]; then
    echo "audit: no events found in range (nothing to reconcile)"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "audit: $_checked event(s) all produced their edge"
  return 0
}

_U="usage: vigilant go <open|lock|sleep|suspend> [atleast]
                 | force <state> | only <state> | status
                 | verify [edge] | hooks [edge] | plan [edge]
                 | report | rescue
                 | due [edge] | enforce | audit
       (the wiring audit is 'setup.sh check', not a verb here)"
_sub=${1:-}
if [ "$#" -gt 0 ]; then shift; fi
case "$_sub" in
  go)
    if [ "$#" -lt 1 ]; then
      echo "vigilant: 'go' needs a state" >&2; echo "$_U" >&2; exit 2
    fi
    # AN UNRECOGNISED MODE IS A USAGE ERROR, never something to drop. This arm
    # used to ignore everything after the state, so a caller asking for a mode
    # would have silently got the DEFAULT -- and the default is the one that
    # raises. A safety qualifier that can be misspelt into nothing is not a
    # safety qualifier, and this stack has already paid for one dead config
    # word (`blank-after`) that nothing validated.
    case "${2:-}" in
      ''|atleast) ;;
      *) echo "vigilant: 'go' has no mode '$2'" >&2; echo "$_U" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    cmd_go "$1" "${2:-}" ;;
  force)
    if [ "$#" -lt 1 ]; then
      echo "vigilant: 'force' needs a state" >&2; echo "$_U" >&2; exit 2
    fi
    cmd_go "$1" force ;;
  only)
    if [ "$#" -lt 1 ]; then
      echo "vigilant: 'only' needs a state" >&2; echo "$_U" >&2; exit 2
    fi
    cmd_go "$1" only ;;
  plan)    cmd_plan "${1:-}" ;;
  status)  cmd_status ;;
  verify)  cmd_verify "${1:-}" ;;
  hooks)   cmd_hooks "${1:-}" ;;
  report)  cmd_report ;;
  rescue)  cmd_rescue ;;
  due)     cmd_due "${1:-}" ;;
  enforce) cmd_enforce ;;
  audit)   cmd_audit ;;
  # NAMED EXPLICITLY rather than falling through to "unknown command", because
  # three documents promised this verb and somebody will type it.
  check)
    echo "vigilant: there is no 'check' here; the wiring audit is" >&2
    echo "  'setup.sh check' (it owns the prefix, deps and plugin tree)." >&2
    echo "  For state-vs-hardware, you want 'vigilant report'." >&2
    exit 2 ;;
  -h|--help|help) echo "$_U" ;;
  *) echo "vigilant: unknown command '${_sub:-}'" >&2; echo "$_U" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
